WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.100
- Welcome everyone,
I'm Jacqueline Goldsby,

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.640
I'm Chair of the African
American Studies Department.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.750
And it's really, really wonderful
to be able to welcome you

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:20.750
to the first in a series
of Endeavors Colloquiums

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.570
for this semester.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.320
I won't let it go without
saying that I wish

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.330
we could be gathering
together in physical,

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:34.470
real time space with each other
at the department's building

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.350
on campus 81 Wall Street in
the Gordon Park Seminar room

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.970
where we traditionally hold
these thought, gatherings,

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.954
these think tanks,

00:00:43.954 --> 00:00:47.510
but we are where we are with the pandemic

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:51.030
and the ways in which Zoom
actually makes different

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.680
kinds of convenings possible,

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.290
that actually have led to
having some exciting thought

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:58.540
leaders here with us today,

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.250
so we will run with the virtual space

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.730
but I don't wanna let it go without saying

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:07.800
that we would traditionally
be holding this

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.290
in our department building,

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.407
but we are in the Zoom space.

00:01:11.407 --> 00:01:15.230
And I just want to point
out a few logistical points

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:16.710
for all of us to get today.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:21.650
You should have received the
message that we are recording

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:25.130
this session and we will
be posting it online

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:28.130
so that it's available to anyone

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.000
to think with the ideas that
are gonna be raised here today.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:31.900
But for that reason,

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.700
we would ask that you mute your mics

00:01:35.029 --> 00:01:37.500
so that we can have a
kind of clean soundtrack

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.870
for the recording.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:41.930
And also when we turn to Q and A,

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.920
but as the discussion evolves and moves,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.910
feel free to post your
questions, your comments,

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.380
your thoughts in the chat

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.200
and our facilitator for today
will be tracking that space

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.520
and bring your ideas and bring your voices

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.023
into the discussion when we
hit that part of the program.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:04.230
I just wanna flag for us
that this is the first

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.710
in the series of colloquium

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:08.473
that we'll be having this spring,

00:02:09.518 --> 00:02:10.970
and remind us all

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:14.030
and especially for those of
you who are new and our guests,

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:16.410
that there are a couple of unique features

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.000
about the Endeavor series
that's worth naming

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.160
since we're coming back together

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:25.770
after not holding the
series last semester.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.010
One thing about our department colloquium

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.960
is that it's department wide.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:34.430
In other departments tend
to organize by sub-fields,

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.700
but we bring everyone together

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.370
because in African-American Studies,

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:43.120
we approach questions and
issues and plan making

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.250
we want to pursue
interdisciplinarily, right,

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:48.470
bringing the disciplines together.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:52.440
And so we bring everyone
in this shared space.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.740
And another feature about our colloquium

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:59.680
is that it's a space for
incubating and fostering new ideas,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.160
new art, new scholarship,

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.050
new ways to be engaged in community work.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:08.670
And so we're really excited
to be thinking at the edge

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:12.530
of what's happening in our
respective disciplines,

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:16.270
but together to move the new ideas,

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:20.793
to push the endeavors out,
hints the title of our series.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.130
I wanna point out that this
semester we're focusing

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.980
on breakthrough ideas,
breakthrough models,

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.740
breakthrough provocations,
they are coming to us

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.560
from the social sciences.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.300
And so we'll be convening sociologists,

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:39.560
political scientists,
psychologists, economists,

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.530
anthropologists, to
really push our thinking

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:44.380
about the leading issues

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.170
that are shaping Black life worlds today.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.160
And so I just wanna mark
some future dates for you.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.850
On March 31st, my
colleague Professor Amy Cox

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.760
will facilitate a panel discussion

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.290
on the anthropology of abolition,

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.750
which will bring together
are really exciting

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:03.070
anthropologists who are setting

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:05.900
the carceral state and state violence.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.790
And then we're looking and
finalizing panels for April

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:13.910
where we'll focus on
race in higher education.

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:16.120
And the case for reparations,
which is, you know,

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.650
at Yale is a very live discussion

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:21.670
given the work of the Yale
and slavery working group.

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:24.370
So those panels will
be happening in April,

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:26.420
and as soon as we confirm the speakers

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:27.300
we'll let you know,

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.720
but do mark March 31st,

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:32.183
where we'll have Savannah saying change,

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:36.130
Christian Smith and Deborah Thomas with us

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:39.310
to talk about the
anthropology of abolition.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:42.830
Today's panel on Black activism
amidst community violence

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:46.650
will be facilitated by my
colleague, Professor Philip Goff.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.990
And I'll hand it over to Professor Goff

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:50.640
to introduce our panelists today.

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:53.363
- Thank you so much, Jackie,

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.810
for those introductory remarks

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.350
and for all of your
leadership of this department

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:01.730
that allows us to have
these kinds of conversation,

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:04.450
wanted to make sure that my
mute off button has worked.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:07.410
So can I get a thumbs up
that from the panelists

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.493
that folks are hearing
me, thank you so much.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:14.920
It's a rare pleasure
in doing academic work

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.390
that you get to hear from national leaders

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.240
together in conversation.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.470
That's part of what we're
trying to do this semester

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:25.270
to in certain part compensate for Zoom,

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:28.720
and to create a little bit
more sense of community.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:31.340
So it's a rare pleasure
that we get to do that.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:34.120
It is also a rare pleasure
to be able to do that

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:36.300
while talking to people
I've considered friends

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:37.400
for a number of years.

00:05:38.402 --> 00:05:40.600
So I'm mostly going to
want to get out of the way,

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:42.440
I'm gonna do a little bit of housekeeping

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:45.630
and do a little bit of
sort of shaping of it.

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.740
But I mostly want you all to get a chance

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:51.140
to experience the genius
we have been so fortunate

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.730
to collect for today.

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:57.580
The little bit of housekeeping as Jackie

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.363
had mentioned previously,

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:04.100
if you can please mute your
video and your mic throughout

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:05.890
because we are doing a recording.

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:07.470
If you've got questions, please, please,

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.700
please, put it in the chat.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:11.640
And I will go ahead and
facilitate that when it comes time

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.180
to the Q and A, and for
ease of your own viewing,

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:17.090
if you would like we
encourage you to go ahead

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:20.900
and put the presentation on speaker view,

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:22.173
just as we move through.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.340
With that, let me go ahead
and frame this conversation

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.330
that I couldn't be more excited
to just be listening to,

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.450
I know I gotta facilitate it too,

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:36.450
which is this, the past two
years have seen the largest

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:41.470
single set of protests in
human history by all accounts.

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:44.210
They were around racial injustice,

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:46.560
public safety and policing
systems in the United States,

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:48.363
but they went global.

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:52.300
Those protests and the
organizing and activism

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:55.220
that surrounded them that
was in response to them

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:57.120
has not stopped.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.120
And yet at the same time, the levers,

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:07.140
the pendulum of sort of
racial momentum have shifted

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:13.230
in those two years from one
of our international appetite

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:15.120
for significant change,

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:16.540
not just in criminal legal systems,

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:19.650
but of course all the systems
and that feed into them

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.170
to one where just today,

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:25.150
we're hearing stories
that all of those protests

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:30.010
in fact were a fools errand
that they have gone far too far

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.530
and that they have asked
for and even pushed,

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:35.920
achieved too much.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:37.050
The reality of course,

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:40.190
is that while protests were massive

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:44.000
and demands were radically
sort of oriented,

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.630
the amount that has
changed is much smaller.

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:49.960
And that is in the national narrative.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:52.650
And all of that is within the context

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:57.650
of a controversially
affiliated spike, not in crime,

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:04.380
but in murder, in violence
within communities.

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.950
And in fact, it is that spike in violence

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:10.100
within communities that has
led many folks to say, well,

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:14.280
these new demands, these new
policies must be at fault.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.180
We must go back to way the
things were, this is overreach.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:22.970
First, let me say that
we have to acknowledge

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.713
the murder spike is real.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:27.990
It is not some form of propaganda.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:29.740
It is not faked data.

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:31.810
I specialize in identifying fake data,

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:34.630
particularly in public safety
and the most reliable data

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.310
that you can get ever from law enforcement

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:39.323
are those that can be double
checked at the morgue.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.710
Black communities have been and hurting

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:43.840
in the midst of the last two years,

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:45.330
not just from the pandemic,

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.700
not just from the economic
consequences thereof,

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.223
but from community violence.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.810
And yet there is written nowhere,

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:54.940
a law that says it is in fact

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:57.570
when violence is at its
heightened communities,

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:00.600
that we must rely on punitive institutions

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:02.220
to remedy it, right?

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:04.400
The argument can easily
be made if police budgets

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.110
where at their height in the
midst of this while on earth,

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:10.170
where we need to increase policing, right?

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:13.640
There is significant question
about where and when and how

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:15.760
policing as opposed to
community empowerment

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:18.480
drives down that violence,
prevents that violence,

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:19.403
even predicts it?

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:23.750
These questions are in the
context of the long struggle

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.220
for Black liberation,

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:27.920
the tension between what
do we need to do right now,

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:30.020
amidst the hell that
Black folks are catching

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:31.380
and what do we need to
plan for in the future,

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:33.560
so that there is a future where
we're not demanding justice,

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:35.160
but we are experiencing freedom?

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:37.793
How do we move within that?

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.580
What are the appropriate
roles for academia?

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:45.040
And what do we see as the trends,

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:47.360
the promises within activism organizing

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:49.590
and a collective united voice

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:51.800
to demand changes of the experiences

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:53.530
that we're having right now?

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:57.260
I could think of no better group of humans

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:58.680
to talk to us about that,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:02.270
than the four assembled for us
who will then go on to solve

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:05.810
all of these problems,
no pressure, low bar.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:09.410
We're gonna be hearing
from in order Nikki Jones,

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:13.950
who is Professor and H. Michael
and Jean Williams Department

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:16.010
Chair of African-American Studies

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:19.033
at the African-American Studies
Department at UC Berkeley.

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:25.170
She is one of the, if not per
generations, my generations,

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:29.150
greatest ethnographers of the
forms of invisible violence

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:30.710
we have done to Black people,

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:33.880
generally Black women
and girls in particular

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:36.903
that is invisible that she makes visible.

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:41.460
She's awarded the American
Society of Criminology,

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:43.310
outstanding research award in 2020.

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:45.710
Her list goes on and on.

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:48.790
I encourage you to read
"Between Good and Ghetto,"

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:51.800
her first book, as well as
her many, many articles,

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:53.500
the regular routine, all of them.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.150
These are staples in my classes

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:57.430
and have radically shaped the way

00:10:57.430 --> 00:10:59.030
in which I think about these issues.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:00.493
She will speak first.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.850
Second will be Patrick Sharkey,

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:07.350
who is the William S. Todd
Professor of Sociology

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.620
and Public Affairs at
Princeton University.

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:13.710
Perhaps no one has written
as careful and nuanced

00:11:14.646 --> 00:11:16.260
a take on what is happening

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:20.283
in particularly urban pockets
of violence than Pat Sharkey.

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:24.420
I can and will go on and on,

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:26.780
but he is perhaps most notably for this

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:28.240
if folks are looking for the website,

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:30.470
the Founder of americanviolence.org,

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:32.780
go there, it is an amazing resource,

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:35.460
and I cannot tell you how excited I am

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:37.110
that he is here to speak with us.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:40.200
Third, Brandon Terry,

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:42.660
who is a Presidential visiting
Assistant Professor here

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:43.493
at Yale this series.

00:11:43.493 --> 00:11:46.020
And he is an Assistant Professor
of African-American Studies

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:48.590
and American Studies and of
Social Studies at Harvard,

00:11:48.590 --> 00:11:51.160
'cause he likes to have
multiple affiliations.

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:54.080
He is a preeminent thinker and scholar

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:57.200
of Black radical political fault.

00:11:57.200 --> 00:11:58.190
And I'm excited to hear

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:00.370
how he's going to be framing this moment

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:02.030
in terms of how Black leadership

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:06.750
can and should be a
continuation and a departure

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:09.140
of the legacies that we bring with us.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:11.760
And last but certainly not
least Pastor Michael McBride

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:13.550
is a native side of San Francisco,

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:16.050
he got a ministry in
Oakland for over 20 years.

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:18.950
Pastor McBride is the Executive
Director of Live Free USA,

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:21.660
a nonprofit committed
to ending gun violence.

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:24.550
He is an intellectual
leader, a movement leader,

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:27.763
a moral leader on these
issues and well beyond.

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:29.920
So mostly what I wanna do,

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:32.250
as I said is get the heck out of the way

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:35.790
to let these folk rip on
giving us some more wisdom

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:37.720
with regards to these issues

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:40.200
where the pendulum has
not only swung back,

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:43.460
but it has sharp edges and
it is pushing us further away

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:46.360
from where we're trying
to go than we were before

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:50.040
the public lynching of
George Floyd May 25th, 2020.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:53.150
Dr. Nikki Jones, could you
please offer some initial remarks

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:55.180
before we get to the Q and A?
- Sure, sure.

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:56.550
- And I'll proceed to each of them,

00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:57.740
each folks after that.

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:00.780
- Yeah, and thank you, Professor
Goff for that introduction,

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:03.230
for the invitation to be here.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:04.680
Thank you, Vanessa,

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:06.430
for all the work that
you did behind the scenes

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:08.910
to get us in this virtual space together.

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:10.880
And thank you Professor Goldsby

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:12.660
for those introductory comments

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:15.810
and the invitation to think together.

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:19.650
I'm joining you here from
Berkeley unseated alone

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:23.450
in the territory in the Bay Area.

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:24.960
I'm joining you at a moment

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:28.430
that is really a reflective moment for me

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:30.800
and thinking about what we've learned

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:33.950
from the uprisings of summer 2020,

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.450
especially context of policing and working

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:39.910
in the area of policing
over the course of my career

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:43.000
and seeing for the first
time in our history

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:46.560
where we are seeing the pairing,

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:50.570
the intertwining of uprisings
against systemic racism

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:51.740
and police violence,

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:53.780
which is much different
even from seven years ago,

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:55.900
the call for police reforms.

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:59.090
And so that has been
very clarifying for me.

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:01.870
So thinking deeply about
what Black activists

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:03.680
have always encouraged us us to do

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:06.920
is to see things more clearly
to sharpen our vision,

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:08.957
to better understand
and define the problem.

00:14:08.957 --> 00:14:11.970
And so what are the things
that I know with more certainty

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:13.910
because of the work that I've done

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:14.990
over the course of my career

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:17.130
and because of this particular moment.

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:18.287
Now first we know,

00:14:18.287 --> 00:14:20.030
and I think more people understand

00:14:20.030 --> 00:14:21.950
that policing is not one thing.

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:24.150
And so we often talk about
policing a bit large,

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:26.600
but I think if we just think
about the media coverage

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:28.030
of the "Black Lives Matter" protest

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:30.490
of the uprisings of summer 2020,

00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:32.460
and the violence that
was freely distributed

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:33.940
upon protestors,

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:37.260
and then we think about the
insurrection on January 6th,

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:39.680
and people trying to make
sense of what they're seeing

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:42.383
and the reserve in which violence was held

00:14:42.383 --> 00:14:43.940
when the mob, right,

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:45.480
when the, the uprising,

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:48.260
the insurrection was led by White people

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:51.530
and to large degree White nationalist.

00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:55.960
And so more folks understood
that policing is not the same,

00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:57.730
always, but certainly in
the work that I've done.

00:14:57.730 --> 00:15:00.740
I have understood that
both in the epigraphic work

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:02.550
and certainly came to appreciate that more

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:04.850
in the work that I've done interviewing

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:06.920
and studying police officers

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:10.730
who very freely share that
policing is not one thing,

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:13.100
they don't police neighborhoods the same

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:15.380
in that the brunt of that
violence and aggression

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:17.380
often comes down on the
bodies of Black youth

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:19.330
like those I've spent time with

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:20.853
over the course of my career.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:24.540
I think this moment is also
called on us to think critically

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:27.060
about policing as a response to violence

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:30.100
in a way that that is much more refined,

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:32.240
I think, than we have previously.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:34.650
And to better appreciate
an argument that I make

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:36.137
in my most recent book,

00:15:36.137 --> 00:15:39.150
"The Chosen Ones" that policing
acts as a co-constructor

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:41.580
of violence and that's not hyperbole,

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:43.370
and it's not even a radical position

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:45.180
to say that policing is about violence.

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:49.310
Policing scholars have
said this for 50+ years

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:51.467
that violence is central to policing.

00:15:51.467 --> 00:15:55.630
And in the course of my
work as Phil alluded to,

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:59.170
I certainly, I am thinking
about the extraordinary forms

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:01.260
of violence and the spectacles of violence

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:02.417
that we've come to to see,

00:16:02.417 --> 00:16:04.270
and that motivate people.

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:05.630
But I've spent a lot of time

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:08.330
to thinking about the ordinary violence,

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:10.880
the routine violence in
the way that the body

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:13.300
becomes an archive for that violence.

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:14.710
The body becomes conditioned.

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:17.050
The bodies of young
people become conditioned

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:17.883
to that violence.

00:16:17.883 --> 00:16:20.330
And I think we have other
archives of that as well.

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:22.730
We can think about pattern
and practice investigations,

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:25.550
for example, and people
often, you know, wanna know,

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:28.140
is this a kind of reform
that can change policing?

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:30.910
And I take a slightly
different look at these records

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:33.470
and think about the ways
in which it's an archive

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:34.730
of the routine violence,

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:38.350
of the ordinary violence that
is common until it's not,

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:41.040
until it's made extraordinary by an act.

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:42.780
But then if you look back,

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:46.720
you see that the record of
routine violence was there.

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:47.890
And if that's the case, then, you know,

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:51.160
I think we have to think
critically about not the role

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:55.300
of policing and responding
to this murder spike,

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:56.740
this spike in violence,

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:58.360
but what is the role of state violence

00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:00.007
in responding to violence?

00:17:00.007 --> 00:17:02.540
And what are the limits of that?

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:07.450
And so certainly thinking
about the kind of not expanding

00:17:07.450 --> 00:17:10.410
the narrow way in which we
often thought about policing

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:15.410
as a response or as a
provider of public safety.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:17.123
And then I think this moment,

00:17:17.123 --> 00:17:19.020
well I hope we have learned

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:24.020
and what I know folks are
pushing us to appreciate

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:26.630
is the way that this moment is an echo.

00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:29.640
It's an echo of earlier times
going back centuries, right?

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:32.660
But it is an echo in this
moment of very recent history

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:34.610
of the 80s and 90s, right.

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:39.330
Where we have these racialized
fears around empirical facts.

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:41.970
So just like Professor Goff
said, yes, there is this spike.

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:43.497
How do we respond to that?

00:17:43.497 --> 00:17:45.930
And what is the role of racialized fear?

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:49.760
So certainly the kind
of the racialized images

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:52.440
of Black criminality
are coming to the fore,

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:57.040
whether they're explicitly
addressed or not.

00:17:57.040 --> 00:17:59.250
And it makes it difficult for us

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:01.447
to really appreciate the
depth of the problem.

00:18:01.447 --> 00:18:03.690
And so certainly we
think about gun violence

00:18:03.690 --> 00:18:06.400
as concentrated in cities
and among Black youth

00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:07.470
and Latinx youth.

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:09.220
And yet we know that there's been increase

00:18:09.220 --> 00:18:11.137
in domestic violence as well, right?

00:18:11.137 --> 00:18:12.910
And that that's gun violence,

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:15.200
but that's not the vision
that we are bringing

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:17.420
when we have this kind
of really narrow way

00:18:17.420 --> 00:18:18.297
of thinking about the problem,

00:18:18.297 --> 00:18:20.450
and it's intentionally narrow, right.

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:24.380
Because it's not actually
about addressing the problem,

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:26.820
it's about politicizing the problem

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:28.320
and certainly political power.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:33.540
So those are a few things
that are more clarified for me

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:34.373
moving through this moment.

00:18:34.373 --> 00:18:35.280
And then I appreciate,

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:38.010
and I just wanna, so I'll
close, I'll stop there for now,

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:39.147
but just to say that, you know,

00:18:39.147 --> 00:18:42.090
I'm really looking forward
to being in this conversation

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:45.423
and learning from the
other panelists, thank you.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:48.680
- Thank you so much, Dr. Jones.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:51.960
Dr. Sharkey, could you speak
to the people a little bit?

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:52.793
- Yeah, I'd love to.

00:18:52.793 --> 00:18:55.460
Thanks for having me with this group.

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:59.270
Man, I've learned so
much from Dr. Jones work.

00:18:59.270 --> 00:19:01.620
It's great to be on the same panel

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:05.560
and to be at Yale where I
think some of the best work,

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:08.623
probably the best work on around
these issues is happening.

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:15.130
So I started studying violence
because the more evidence

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:20.810
I looked at, the more I
began to think of violence

00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:24.400
as central to understanding
inequality in the U.S

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:26.680
and that's reciprocal.

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:30.500
So urban inequality creates
the conditions for violence

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:34.650
to emerge by concentrating
disadvantage in the U.S,

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:37.578
by bringing about sustained disinvestment

00:19:37.578 --> 00:19:41.330
in core community institutions,

00:19:41.330 --> 00:19:45.290
by leading to a system of punishment.

00:19:45.290 --> 00:19:47.340
And that's U.S specific as well.

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:48.880
But through these mechanisms,

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:50.870
urban inequality creates the conditions

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:52.770
for violent to emerge,

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:57.280
but then violence amplifies
and reinforces inequality.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:59.950
And it does that directly
by taking young lives,

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:04.800
but it also does that by
impeding academic performance

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:09.800
and achievement by influencing
the economic outcomes

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:14.080
of young people, by
enhancing mental illness,

00:20:14.080 --> 00:20:17.350
by undermining communities.

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:20.980
So that's the frame at which
I started to see violence

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:23.810
as fundamental to understanding
inequality in the U.S.

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:25.613
And then the question is, well,

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:30.230
when we look at what has
happened since the 1990s,

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:35.230
so violence fell by roughly
half from early 1990s to 2014.

00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:37.240
So what happened?

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:38.853
What brought about that change?

00:20:39.830 --> 00:20:44.600
And that's why I started
studying community organizations,

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:46.710
the movements that were
happening in neighborhoods

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:48.020
across the country.

00:20:48.020 --> 00:20:50.870
So I think it was 2015,

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:53.300
I sat down with three graduate students,

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:56.290
basically because we were
unsatisfied with the explanations

00:20:56.290 --> 00:21:00.950
for why violence fell, read
all these inspiring books

00:21:00.950 --> 00:21:05.500
about organizations and
people who had mobilized

00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:09.010
to retake public spaces and make them safe

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:13.710
for communities to march
against violence to mobilize,

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:16.620
to provide services for mental illness,

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:19.130
for addiction to provide basic services

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:20.800
like after school programs

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:22.330
and communities that were hit hardest

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:25.340
by the rise of violence
in the 70s and 80s.

00:21:26.269 --> 00:21:28.460
And so when we looked at national data,

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:31.190
we were basically trying
to figure out, okay,

00:21:31.190 --> 00:21:33.490
we have these explanations
for why violence fell,

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:35.410
what else was happening on the ground?

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:37.550
And we looked, we gathered all the data

00:21:37.550 --> 00:21:41.360
that we could in cities
across the country.

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:44.820
And we found the stuff that
other people had found.

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:47.513
So there was a large scale
increase in police force,

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:52.090
there were changes in police tactics.

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:55.040
There was a continued
rise in incarceration.

00:21:55.040 --> 00:21:57.820
There was an expansion of surveillance

00:21:57.820 --> 00:22:00.910
and closed circuit camera
systems implemented

00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:03.870
in cities across the country.

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:06.270
But there was also
something else that we saw.

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:09.910
There was a mobilization
of the nonprofit sector

00:22:09.910 --> 00:22:12.090
that occurred at a national level.

00:22:12.090 --> 00:22:15.170
So these stories are told
in terms of heroic acts

00:22:15.170 --> 00:22:17.780
of individuals or were
specific groups of people

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:19.230
in a given neighborhood,

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:20.410
and they should be told that way,

00:22:20.410 --> 00:22:21.580
they are heroic acts.

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:26.526
This is my comments are
not meant to diminish

00:22:26.526 --> 00:22:29.880
the incredible work that's going on

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:31.590
in neighborhoods across country.

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:33.490
But what I would point out

00:22:33.490 --> 00:22:36.100
is that this was a national mobilization

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:38.360
that took place in the early 1990s,

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:39.690
the nonprofit sector,

00:22:39.690 --> 00:22:42.970
particularly organizations
that were formed

00:22:42.970 --> 00:22:43.940
to deal with violence,

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:46.100
but also just to build
stronger communities,

00:22:46.100 --> 00:22:48.540
you can see in the national data,

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:49.890
there's proliferation.

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:52.990
So what we did in that first study,

00:22:52.990 --> 00:22:55.550
where I looked at the impact

00:22:55.550 --> 00:22:58.840
of this mobilization of
nonprofit organizations,

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:01.930
was to try to identify causal evidence,

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:04.100
to see whether there was a causal impact

00:23:04.100 --> 00:23:07.870
of the expansion of the nonprofit
sector on violent crime.

00:23:07.870 --> 00:23:11.727
And in that first study that we did,

00:23:11.727 --> 00:23:14.070
Delaram Takyar was one
of the graduate students,

00:23:14.070 --> 00:23:18.240
Gerard Torrats-Espinoza was
another and Kiera Darts.

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:21.303
I wanna make sure to give them all credit.

00:23:23.070 --> 00:23:24.300
What we found is that,

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:26.740
so we exploited kind of shocks in funding

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:29.220
that led to more nonprofits emerging

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:30.650
to identify the causal impact.

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:32.220
What we found is that in a typical city,

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:34.540
a city with about 100,000 people,

00:23:34.540 --> 00:23:38.550
every 10 additional
nonprofits that were formed

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:42.010
to deal with violence,
to retake public spaces,

00:23:42.010 --> 00:23:44.240
to provide services, to provide housing,

00:23:44.240 --> 00:23:46.210
to provide after school programs,

00:23:46.210 --> 00:23:49.520
led to about a 9% drop in the murder rate.

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:52.440
The implication of this
is that in addition

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:55.020
to all those other changes
that get so much attention

00:23:55.020 --> 00:23:58.120
about shifts in policing and
incarceration and so forth,

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:00.310
there was a mobilization that took place

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:02.920
in the communities hit
hardest by violence.

00:24:02.920 --> 00:24:05.990
That mobilization expansion of nonprofits

00:24:05.990 --> 00:24:08.430
played a central role in contributing

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:12.570
to the fall of violence from
the 1990s to the mid 2010s.

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:15.790
The reason that's important
is because when you think

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:19.190
about the explanations
for why violence fell,

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:21.190
or when you think about the reactions

00:24:21.190 --> 00:24:23.330
to the recent rise in violence

00:24:23.330 --> 00:24:25.730
in the U.S, we immediately go back

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:29.160
to this knee jerk reaction
and think about a response

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:31.660
that is focused on the
institutions of punishment,

00:24:31.660 --> 00:24:33.460
the police and the prison system,

00:24:33.460 --> 00:24:35.360
that is the default response

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:37.380
to arise of violence in the U.S.

00:24:37.380 --> 00:24:41.460
This evidence provides a proof of concept

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:44.170
that a different approach is necessary.

00:24:44.170 --> 00:24:47.810
The groups that mobilized in the 1990s

00:24:47.810 --> 00:24:50.000
are groups that were underfunded,

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:52.650
that did not have national support

00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:54.410
that were not seen as central actors

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:55.860
in the effort to deal with violence,

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:58.550
and yet they had this enormous impact

00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:00.100
on the decline in violence.

00:25:00.100 --> 00:25:02.210
They played a central role in that effort.

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:03.820
So this observation is reinforced

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:06.400
by a whole bunch of
randomized control trials.

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:09.400
The evidence base has grown
stronger and stronger,

00:25:09.400 --> 00:25:13.010
and what it tells us is
that we have had a model

00:25:13.010 --> 00:25:16.060
for a long time that assumes

00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:18.870
that to deal with rising
violence we have to turn back

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:21.730
to the institutions of
punishment in the U.S.

00:25:21.730 --> 00:25:23.558
There is another model,

00:25:23.558 --> 00:25:26.010
the groups and the people

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:28.000
that have always had the greatest capacity

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:31.080
to control violence or
organizations that are motivated

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:32.740
by building stronger neighborhoods,

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:35.051
by looking out for their neighbors,

00:25:35.051 --> 00:25:37.080
making sure everybody's
welcome in their community,

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:39.190
making sure their communities stay strong

00:25:39.190 --> 00:25:41.260
and no one falls through the cracks.

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:42.170
Those organizations,

00:25:42.170 --> 00:25:45.190
we now know we have enough evidence now

00:25:45.190 --> 00:25:48.309
to know that those organizations
have the greatest capacity

00:25:48.309 --> 00:25:50.030
to control violence,

00:25:50.030 --> 00:25:52.530
and yet they've just never
been given the commitment

00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:55.870
and the resources to do
so in a sustained way.

00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:56.830
So I think, you know,

00:25:56.830 --> 00:26:00.810
the main message here is
that we can make a case

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:03.070
for the work that Pastor Mike is doing

00:26:03.070 --> 00:26:05.720
the work that's going
on around the country,

00:26:05.720 --> 00:26:08.642
on the merits of justice,

00:26:08.642 --> 00:26:12.530
we can praise the work that's going on,

00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:14.320
the incredible hard work that's going on

00:26:14.320 --> 00:26:16.180
without sufficient resources,

00:26:16.180 --> 00:26:19.030
but we can also make an empirical case.

00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:23.960
This work is not only how I
think the world should work,

00:26:23.960 --> 00:26:26.760
it is backed up by extraordinary
evidence at this point.

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:30.920
- Thank you, Dr. Sharkey.

00:26:30.920 --> 00:26:32.970
Dr. Terry, you wanna speak to the people?

00:26:34.910 --> 00:26:36.070
- So first off,

00:26:36.070 --> 00:26:39.390
I want to thank Phil for convening us here

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:42.420
for inviting me really
honored to be in conversation

00:26:42.420 --> 00:26:44.320
with you all who have learned so much

00:26:44.320 --> 00:26:46.340
from and been inspired by.

00:26:46.340 --> 00:26:47.680
I'm a political theorist.

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:49.110
So I come at these questions

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:51.440
from a bit of a different vantage,

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:56.230
and I wanted to open up
by speaking forthrightly

00:26:56.230 --> 00:26:58.510
of what I think is a
kind of ghost haunting,

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:01.550
these kinds of discussions
about community violence.

00:27:01.550 --> 00:27:03.430
And that's the older idea

00:27:03.430 --> 00:27:06.790
or concept of Black on Black crime.

00:27:06.790 --> 00:27:09.250
Community violence is arrayed productively

00:27:09.250 --> 00:27:11.910
against this earlier once prominent idea.

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:14.440
And it's an attempt to replace race based

00:27:14.440 --> 00:27:17.090
with police based arguments.

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:20.070
But for someone like me who
was formed in many respects

00:27:20.070 --> 00:27:21.620
by the 1990s period,

00:27:21.620 --> 00:27:23.780
that Pat is talking a about,

00:27:23.780 --> 00:27:26.220
the decline of Black on Black crime talk

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:27.870
is really fascinating.

00:27:27.870 --> 00:27:30.270
So it was once a familiar
part of Black liberal,

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:32.320
nationalist and radical discourse.

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:35.280
But now it's principally
used by conservative critics

00:27:35.280 --> 00:27:37.110
of "Black Lives Matter,"

00:27:37.110 --> 00:27:38.750
writers like Ta-Nehisi Coates,

00:27:38.750 --> 00:27:42.340
decry its use as ideological and cynical.

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:45.360
They say it represents an
illicit changing of the subject

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:48.330
against legitimate demands
of anti-racist activists.

00:27:48.330 --> 00:27:51.020
And sociologically, I think the consensus

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:54.450
is that Black on Black violence concepts,

00:27:54.450 --> 00:27:58.290
lead analysts to confuse the
effects of racial segregation

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:01.180
for a unique pathology of Black people,

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:04.010
think it unfairly racializes crime,

00:28:04.010 --> 00:28:06.440
further stigmatizes Blacks,

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:08.630
and perhaps even more devastating

00:28:08.630 --> 00:28:10.890
is that the concept seems to suggest

00:28:10.890 --> 00:28:12.840
the need for an explanation

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:15.340
of why to quote Alvin Poussaint's

00:28:15.340 --> 00:28:19.700
book title from the 1970s,
"Blacks Kill Blacks."

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:22.830
And this peculiar demand
for an explanation

00:28:22.830 --> 00:28:27.660
tends to generate odd Black
psychology in Poussaint form,

00:28:27.660 --> 00:28:29.630
it drew on the "Wretched of the Earth"

00:28:29.630 --> 00:28:33.207
and he wrote things like "By
attacking a fellow Black,

00:28:33.207 --> 00:28:36.267
"we may feel that we are
committing a manly act,

00:28:36.267 --> 00:28:38.357
"the destruction of sin itself.

00:28:38.357 --> 00:28:40.547
"We may express our self rejection

00:28:40.547 --> 00:28:43.810
"by acting out the White racist wish."

00:28:43.810 --> 00:28:47.740
So this idea of internalized oppression,

00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:51.250
despite its origins and
Black power radicalism

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:54.470
licensed surprising
support for authoritarian

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:58.530
and violent repression of
Black crime and Black people

00:28:58.530 --> 00:29:01.560
as Black radicals themselves
became more despairing

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:03.350
of the prospects for the revolution

00:29:03.350 --> 00:29:06.950
and consciousness that might
shed these opposed pathologies

00:29:06.950 --> 00:29:09.050
through politics.

00:29:09.050 --> 00:29:13.080
Now, given the flaws of
the picture I just painted,

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:16.420
it's easy to narrate the turn
to the community violence

00:29:16.420 --> 00:29:19.700
concept as progressive
and an instance of genuine

00:29:19.700 --> 00:29:21.560
sociological learning.

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:25.230
But I think if we're too
triumphant about that story,

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:28.200
I think we miss something
about the popularity

00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:30.530
of Black on Black crime discourse

00:29:30.530 --> 00:29:32.440
in Black communities that might help us

00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:35.240
to produce a still more effective theory,

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:37.680
politics and rhetoric in the present.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:40.070
So I think there's a
slight of hand in the way

00:29:40.070 --> 00:29:43.140
that some analysts conflate
the White dominated discourse

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:45.860
about Black crime from the 1890s

00:29:45.860 --> 00:29:48.230
and its obsessions with
Black on White crime,

00:29:48.230 --> 00:29:51.820
especially rape with the
discourse on Black on Black crime

00:29:51.820 --> 00:29:53.500
a century later,

00:29:53.500 --> 00:29:56.540
the latter was not driven by White racist

00:29:56.540 --> 00:29:58.260
and Black reactionaries alone,

00:29:58.260 --> 00:30:01.520
but was embraced by all
manner of anti-racist artist,

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:04.710
intellectuals activist from Jesse Jackson

00:30:04.710 --> 00:30:06.643
to Sister Souljah to Tupac Shakur.

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:11.510
And what we tend to miss I
think is that Black on Black

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:15.360
crime talk can essentially be
split into two overlapping,

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:18.150
but analytically distinct
families of thought.

00:30:18.150 --> 00:30:21.310
The first which we've discussed
already is sociological

00:30:21.310 --> 00:30:23.960
and explanatory in its aspiration.

00:30:23.960 --> 00:30:26.003
The other however is normative.

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:29.610
It's about ethics and politics.

00:30:29.610 --> 00:30:31.390
So in its normative construction,

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:33.370
Black on Black crime reflects views

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:36.260
about social critique and moral duties.

00:30:36.260 --> 00:30:38.740
The argument was that
Black on Black violence

00:30:38.740 --> 00:30:42.210
was not just a problem,
but a matter of justice,

00:30:42.210 --> 00:30:45.810
it reflects unremedy effects
of broader systemic injustice

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:47.370
and social abandonment.

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:50.100
It intensifies unfair racial stigma

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:53.310
and violates basic rights
of Blacks themselves.

00:30:53.310 --> 00:30:54.360
Further the failure,

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:57.100
the state to effectively
enforce the rule of law

00:30:57.100 --> 00:31:00.640
or exhibit meaningful concern
when Blacks harm other Blacks

00:31:00.640 --> 00:31:03.680
reflects a cultivated
disregard for Black dignity

00:31:03.680 --> 00:31:05.090
and citizenship.

00:31:05.090 --> 00:31:08.760
And as my friend, Tommy Shelby
argues in "Dark Ghettos,"

00:31:08.760 --> 00:31:12.550
such conditions call into
question the very legitimacy

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:15.120
of the whole legal and political order,

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:18.880
as well as the demand
that the oppressed comply

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:21.500
with the law or that they
respect property rights

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:24.163
or contribute to norms of cooperation.

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:27.910
The problem with this critique, however,

00:31:27.910 --> 00:31:31.140
is that it opens up a
dangerous and frightening moral

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:33.010
and political void.

00:31:33.010 --> 00:31:35.560
How can we sustain respect for rights?

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:38.360
How can we sustain cooperative
struggles against injustice?

00:31:38.360 --> 00:31:41.490
How can we sustain collective
survival and flourishing

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:43.643
without the appeal to legitimate law?

00:31:44.550 --> 00:31:45.590
On my view,

00:31:45.590 --> 00:31:49.210
what Black on Black crime talk tried to do

00:31:49.210 --> 00:31:53.340
was use racial solidarity
to fill that void.

00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:54.220
In other words,

00:31:54.220 --> 00:31:56.990
it offers an argument to
those considering violence

00:31:56.990 --> 00:32:00.400
or criminal practices,
which presupposed violence,

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:03.180
and it suggests that such
violence is not just a violation

00:32:03.180 --> 00:32:04.610
of peoples natural rights,

00:32:04.610 --> 00:32:07.370
but violate special solideistic norms,

00:32:07.370 --> 00:32:09.940
like special concern, loyalty,

00:32:09.940 --> 00:32:12.760
the promotion of shared goals and trust.

00:32:12.760 --> 00:32:16.030
These goals, the framework
suggest are necessary

00:32:16.030 --> 00:32:18.690
for the struggle against anti-Black racism

00:32:18.690 --> 00:32:21.590
and the cultivation of
a bearable social life

00:32:21.590 --> 00:32:23.640
amidst injustice.

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:25.890
Black on Black violence on their view

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:30.120
then is a violation of obligations
born of shared oppression

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:31.920
instead of strengthening solidarity,

00:32:31.920 --> 00:32:34.450
it deepens and hardens
patterns of distrust,

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:37.070
makes it harder to
sustain across class ties

00:32:37.070 --> 00:32:39.100
and create psychological
and social traumas

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:41.440
that make the work of
movement building harder.

00:32:41.440 --> 00:32:44.760
It also events as a
kind of cruel parasitism

00:32:44.760 --> 00:32:47.200
amidst shared vulnerability.

00:32:47.200 --> 00:32:49.490
Now in closing, and to reiterate,

00:32:49.490 --> 00:32:52.330
I'm not offering a
defense of the discourse,

00:32:52.330 --> 00:32:54.690
but I'm just pointing out
how different it looks

00:32:54.690 --> 00:32:56.320
from this normative angle,

00:32:56.320 --> 00:32:58.980
as opposed to the explanatory one.

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:01.660
Despite its faults Black
on Black crime talk

00:33:01.660 --> 00:33:04.490
properly reconstructed
seems to take seriously

00:33:04.490 --> 00:33:06.090
a broad suite of challenges

00:33:06.090 --> 00:33:08.220
that I think any replacement discourse

00:33:08.220 --> 00:33:11.740
concerned with Black activism
amidst community violence

00:33:11.740 --> 00:33:13.650
also needs to consider.

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:16.263
I'll close by restating
them quickly as follows.

00:33:17.190 --> 00:33:19.430
The first is a critique of the legitimacy

00:33:19.430 --> 00:33:21.860
of the existing social and legal order

00:33:21.860 --> 00:33:25.880
and a related recognition that
certain forms of law breaking

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:27.903
are forms of legitimate dissent.

00:33:29.290 --> 00:33:32.670
Second is a recognition
that this critique discloses

00:33:32.670 --> 00:33:36.090
a crisis of normativity
that severely dangerous

00:33:36.090 --> 00:33:39.593
to social life and demands a
kind of normative refounding.

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:43.430
Third is a question of
how to be respectful

00:33:43.430 --> 00:33:47.010
of and responsive to the
moral and political agency

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:49.673
of the perpetrators and
victims of violence.

00:33:50.540 --> 00:33:54.720
And last is responding to
existing normative expectations

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:57.163
of Black solidarity
and anti-race struggle,

00:33:58.030 --> 00:33:59.400
anti-race struggle.

00:33:59.400 --> 00:34:01.990
So I think this helps us see more clearly

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:05.430
what was normatively self
undermining about the answers

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:08.060
Black on Black crime
discourse tended to generate

00:34:08.060 --> 00:34:11.360
to the incisive ethical and
political questions it posed,

00:34:11.360 --> 00:34:14.050
it's premona failed to
adequately distinguish

00:34:14.050 --> 00:34:16.310
their normative and explanatory ambitions,

00:34:16.310 --> 00:34:18.040
and they left the concept vulnerable

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:21.180
to use our patient by
authoritarian reactionaries.

00:34:21.180 --> 00:34:24.070
The fixation on the spectacular
character of violence

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:28.040
moreover often degenerated
into what we see as one sided

00:34:28.040 --> 00:34:30.750
demands for solidaristic discipline

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:34.040
expressed by Black elites
against the ghetto poor,

00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:37.740
without equal attention to
the failure of mutual concern

00:34:37.740 --> 00:34:39.110
in the other direction.

00:34:39.110 --> 00:34:40.980
And finally, in most perniciously,

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:43.660
it reflect the roots in Black nationalism

00:34:43.660 --> 00:34:46.670
by permitting the conceptual
partitioning of political space

00:34:46.670 --> 00:34:49.610
and moral responsibility such
that Black on Black crime

00:34:49.610 --> 00:34:53.290
became only a challenge for the
supposed autonomous politics

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:56.100
of Black communities rather
than generating duties

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:58.400
of response for the whole society.

00:34:58.400 --> 00:35:01.610
To the extent that any
community violence paradigm

00:35:01.610 --> 00:35:04.680
failed to pose these
important normative questions

00:35:04.680 --> 00:35:07.430
or reflexively reproduces these flaws,

00:35:07.430 --> 00:35:10.210
I think will undermine some
of the important potential

00:35:10.210 --> 00:35:13.470
gained in moving from
Black on Black crime talk

00:35:13.470 --> 00:35:14.980
to community violence talk

00:35:14.980 --> 00:35:17.950
that we supposedly are experiencing.

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:18.783
I'll stop there.

00:35:20.600 --> 00:35:22.767
- Thank you so much, Dr. Terry,

00:35:22.767 --> 00:35:24.950
and in by way of transition,

00:35:24.950 --> 00:35:28.750
I love to move from the ghosts
of racism to the Holy Ghost.

00:35:28.750 --> 00:35:31.550
So Pastor Mike, you wanna go
ahead and bless the people.

00:35:32.651 --> 00:35:35.420
- Touch your baby with consent.

00:35:35.420 --> 00:35:37.416
When you introduce me
talking about the Holy Ghost.

00:35:37.416 --> 00:35:39.210
I'm gonna get excited now.

00:35:39.210 --> 00:35:40.830
It's great to be here with everyone.

00:35:40.830 --> 00:35:44.260
Obviously, you know,
so much has been said.

00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:45.905
I agree with all of it.

00:35:45.905 --> 00:35:49.770
I will like to introduce my perspective

00:35:49.770 --> 00:35:53.030
or remarks just around this idea

00:35:54.196 --> 00:35:57.340
of my greatest concern.

00:35:57.340 --> 00:35:59.890
This moment presents to me,

00:35:59.890 --> 00:36:03.980
it is around narrative and it
is around how we make meaning

00:36:03.980 --> 00:36:07.850
of the worst conditions in
Black and Brown communities

00:36:08.790 --> 00:36:12.943
in a manner that does not
recycle the very things

00:36:16.650 --> 00:36:20.950
that so many reasonable
people the last several years

00:36:20.950 --> 00:36:23.060
had an epiphany around.

00:36:23.060 --> 00:36:25.950
It is a question to me,
not just a proximity,

00:36:25.950 --> 00:36:27.300
but of empathy.

00:36:27.300 --> 00:36:31.070
It is a question of, do we
believe that Black communities

00:36:31.070 --> 00:36:34.820
belong to the United States of America?

00:36:34.820 --> 00:36:36.930
Or are we a burden?

00:36:36.930 --> 00:36:41.760
And we just want Black folks
and others to die silently

00:36:42.850 --> 00:36:47.850
and invisibly without the
responsibility of the whole nation

00:36:49.310 --> 00:36:53.630
to fix what the nation broke, right?

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:57.030
And I do believe we are in a
crisis moment because you know,

00:36:57.030 --> 00:36:58.260
I'm 46 years old.

00:36:58.260 --> 00:37:02.130
I lived through 90s as a teenager,

00:37:02.130 --> 00:37:06.390
and I got caught up in being
a victim of police brutality.

00:37:06.390 --> 00:37:08.980
I have friends who have
been beat up by cops.

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:11.980
I have friends who've been
shot and killed by guns.

00:37:11.980 --> 00:37:15.530
And so while some may have
the luxury to uncouple

00:37:15.530 --> 00:37:18.717
the issue of policing and quote unquote

00:37:18.717 --> 00:37:20.700
"intra communal violence,"

00:37:20.700 --> 00:37:23.610
the overwhelming majority of Black folks

00:37:23.610 --> 00:37:26.550
caught in these cycles both are directly

00:37:26.550 --> 00:37:30.180
or through proximity
with their family members

00:37:30.180 --> 00:37:33.270
are waking up every
day, trying to navigate

00:37:33.270 --> 00:37:38.270
the existential presence
of violence in their lives

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:42.430
that could come upon them
unexpected with no protection

00:37:42.430 --> 00:37:44.450
and with no security.

00:37:44.450 --> 00:37:46.640
It is really important I
think for us to appreciate

00:37:46.640 --> 00:37:48.900
that it is a false distinction

00:37:48.900 --> 00:37:51.653
between a victim and a perpetrator.

00:37:52.650 --> 00:37:56.810
It is about where you come
into the story of that person,

00:37:56.810 --> 00:38:01.810
will we treat them as the
victimized or the victimizer?

00:38:01.830 --> 00:38:06.480
Violence creates all kinds of
trauma that without therapy

00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:11.210
and support and healing remains
in the soul, the spirit,

00:38:11.210 --> 00:38:14.980
the body, and the mind of the
person who's been victimized.

00:38:14.980 --> 00:38:18.180
And so when we talk about
violence in our communities,

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:21.710
and we try to divide, even
within our own communities,

00:38:21.710 --> 00:38:24.250
folks into good versus bad, you know,

00:38:24.250 --> 00:38:26.170
victim versus perpetrator,

00:38:26.170 --> 00:38:28.450
it really muddies the water in a way

00:38:28.450 --> 00:38:31.580
that I believe caused us to lose out

00:38:31.580 --> 00:38:34.513
on the opportunity to talk
about communal killing,

00:38:35.740 --> 00:38:40.630
communal stabilization and
what must be done to stabilize

00:38:40.630 --> 00:38:43.480
Black communities that
have not spent one day

00:38:43.480 --> 00:38:47.200
in this country and not
experienced state violence

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:51.760
at the hand of largely
White led institutions.

00:38:51.760 --> 00:38:54.010
Now I want you to think about that, right?

00:38:54.010 --> 00:38:55.610
Not one day in this country,

00:38:55.610 --> 00:38:58.850
have we not experienced the violence

00:38:58.850 --> 00:39:00.730
of White man institutions,

00:39:00.730 --> 00:39:03.660
both governmental corporate or otherwise,

00:39:03.660 --> 00:39:07.650
and yet our angst and our retribution

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:11.080
is pointed towards the very folks

00:39:11.080 --> 00:39:13.650
who are being victimized regularly.

00:39:13.650 --> 00:39:16.600
It is as if we are asking Black folks

00:39:16.600 --> 00:39:18.850
who are experiencing
these levels of trauma

00:39:18.850 --> 00:39:21.870
to rise above the trauma
and assure the violence

00:39:21.870 --> 00:39:26.030
that has been thrust upon
us without any resources

00:39:26.030 --> 00:39:29.690
to be healed, to be restored
or to be stabilized.

00:39:29.690 --> 00:39:32.340
And so I do believe that
we have a crisis moment

00:39:32.340 --> 00:39:33.970
around narrative.

00:39:33.970 --> 00:39:38.970
I do think that we all want
to be safe in our communities,

00:39:39.170 --> 00:39:42.500
but the tough on crime
approach has proven to us

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:43.890
to not be the answer.

00:39:43.890 --> 00:39:46.483
There's a scripture that says, you know,

00:39:48.650 --> 00:39:51.040
if you hear the word,
but don't do the word,

00:39:51.040 --> 00:39:53.790
you are like a person who
looks at their reflection

00:39:53.790 --> 00:39:57.870
in the mirror and walks away
and forget what you saw.

00:39:57.870 --> 00:40:01.540
Well, I wanna say that we can't last year,

00:40:01.540 --> 00:40:05.870
I'll talk about we need to
limit the footprint of policing,

00:40:05.870 --> 00:40:08.230
radically reimagined public safety,

00:40:08.230 --> 00:40:10.760
and then allow the conservative forces

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:12.630
largely led by police unions

00:40:12.630 --> 00:40:15.740
who are in their own self description

00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:21.630
supported of the authoritarian
impulses of Donald Trump

00:40:21.630 --> 00:40:22.640
and those forces,

00:40:22.640 --> 00:40:24.840
we want to unleash these individuals

00:40:25.960 --> 00:40:29.470
into our communities to create safety

00:40:29.470 --> 00:40:32.940
while they are all the wild resisting,

00:40:32.940 --> 00:40:36.110
any form of reform and accountability.

00:40:36.110 --> 00:40:38.790
These are the folks we wanna
give have more money to.

00:40:38.790 --> 00:40:42.187
I heard President Biden at
the police foundation say,

00:40:42.187 --> 00:40:43.647
"We don't wanna defund the police

00:40:43.647 --> 00:40:45.730
"we wanna give you more money."

00:40:45.730 --> 00:40:50.180
Why? So you can do therapy, healing.

00:40:50.180 --> 00:40:53.760
We want to ask police
officers to provide therapy

00:40:53.760 --> 00:40:57.470
to Pookie or Ray-Ray
that's the most brilliant

00:40:57.470 --> 00:41:02.470
community safety strategy that
the leader of this country

00:41:03.020 --> 00:41:05.033
is proud to publicly proclaim.

00:41:06.440 --> 00:41:10.550
And then you want folks who
actively work on this every day

00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:13.470
to rob Peter to pay Paul
to sell chicken dinners,

00:41:13.470 --> 00:41:17.610
to sell t-shirts, to fund
a public safety strategy

00:41:17.610 --> 00:41:22.230
that has been 400 years
in the making of a crisis.

00:41:22.230 --> 00:41:25.740
I do believe people that
we must be as serious

00:41:25.740 --> 00:41:29.760
about this problem as we are
about killing Black people

00:41:29.760 --> 00:41:30.593
in this country.

00:41:31.500 --> 00:41:35.010
This is a huge challenge
for our communities

00:41:35.010 --> 00:41:37.490
in the midst of a global pandemic

00:41:38.890 --> 00:41:41.310
that has ravaged the infrastructure,

00:41:41.310 --> 00:41:45.210
the familial ties and
stability of Black communities

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:47.467
and poor communities and many communities.

00:41:47.467 --> 00:41:50.650
And so I do believe the
conversation that we have today

00:41:50.650 --> 00:41:52.960
is a conversation about politics

00:41:52.960 --> 00:41:55.560
and conversation about, you
know, the kind of frameworks

00:41:55.560 --> 00:42:00.463
we use to describe and
to define the phenomena

00:42:00.463 --> 00:42:02.800
that is happening in our communities.

00:42:02.800 --> 00:42:07.577
But it is also about how do
we love Black folks, right?

00:42:08.960 --> 00:42:11.698
How do we love folks into life?

00:42:11.698 --> 00:42:13.337
And I do know now,

00:42:13.337 --> 00:42:15.800
and I'll say a little bit
more of this later on,

00:42:15.800 --> 00:42:17.490
but I do know that there are folks

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:19.870
within the Black community who live

00:42:19.870 --> 00:42:23.130
in very violent communities
and it is they themselves

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:25.220
often calling for more police.

00:42:25.220 --> 00:42:26.810
But I think it's important for us to know

00:42:26.810 --> 00:42:31.020
that we have found strategies
that when people learn

00:42:31.020 --> 00:42:34.310
about these strategies
they actually will ask

00:42:34.310 --> 00:42:36.200
for these strategies as well.

00:42:36.200 --> 00:42:40.730
So the question for us is how
do we mainstream and socialize

00:42:40.730 --> 00:42:43.360
our broader public and
conversation around things

00:42:43.360 --> 00:42:44.680
that not only we know work,

00:42:44.680 --> 00:42:47.080
but that we know community members

00:42:47.080 --> 00:42:50.610
who live in these communities
will actually embrace

00:42:50.610 --> 00:42:54.550
and remove the kind of political anxiety

00:42:54.550 --> 00:42:57.510
of elected officials who claim

00:42:57.510 --> 00:42:59.440
to want to end mass incarceration.

00:42:59.440 --> 00:43:02.040
They claim they want
to end criminalization.

00:43:02.040 --> 00:43:04.960
They claim they want to
end police shootings,

00:43:04.960 --> 00:43:08.860
but don't wanna do anything systemically

00:43:08.860 --> 00:43:10.597
to assist in that process.

00:43:10.597 --> 00:43:13.400
And so I am very grateful to be included

00:43:13.400 --> 00:43:14.633
in this conversation.

00:43:15.490 --> 00:43:17.450
I can go on and on, but I'll stop there

00:43:17.450 --> 00:43:21.370
because I would love to
continue to, you know,

00:43:21.370 --> 00:43:23.360
have the Holy Ghost be activated in me

00:43:23.360 --> 00:43:26.090
by these great scholars
from these prestigious

00:43:27.130 --> 00:43:28.353
wonderful institutions.

00:43:30.346 --> 00:43:32.320
- Did you really end with
Holy Spirit activate?

00:43:32.320 --> 00:43:33.656
Is that what happened, Pastor Mike?

00:43:33.656 --> 00:43:34.970
(Philip laughs)

00:43:34.970 --> 00:43:36.570
- Hey man, no, if it
ain't got that spirit,

00:43:36.570 --> 00:43:38.955
it ain't got that swag, you understand me?

00:43:38.955 --> 00:43:39.788
Come on.

00:43:40.940 --> 00:43:44.180
- Oh, I could not appreciate
the four of y'all more

00:43:45.090 --> 00:43:47.610
what a tremendous set of opening remarks,

00:43:47.610 --> 00:43:49.710
what I'm trying to distill themes.

00:43:49.710 --> 00:43:51.700
And before I get there,
everyone who is here,

00:43:51.700 --> 00:43:52.533
part of the audience,

00:43:52.533 --> 00:43:54.060
we do want you to be able to participate.

00:43:54.060 --> 00:43:56.710
If you've got questions,
please do put them in the chat,

00:43:56.710 --> 00:43:58.070
and I will relay them here

00:43:58.070 --> 00:44:00.260
as part of our ongoing conversation.

00:44:00.260 --> 00:44:03.730
But as I'm pulling out themes
from where Nikki started

00:44:03.730 --> 00:44:05.230
and Pastor Mike where you end,

00:44:06.370 --> 00:44:09.180
one of the themes that
seems like it's very clear

00:44:09.180 --> 00:44:14.180
is that the stories that
we're telling are lies, right?

00:44:14.739 --> 00:44:19.030
The narratives that we've got
insufficient for this moment,

00:44:19.030 --> 00:44:22.600
and there are two examples
that kind of came out to me,

00:44:22.600 --> 00:44:24.890
particularly Brandon, as you were talking,

00:44:24.890 --> 00:44:28.990
one was the tragic story
of Ma'Khia Bryant, right?

00:44:28.990 --> 00:44:32.290
If you recall immediately after
the trial of Derek Chauvin,

00:44:32.290 --> 00:44:36.240
she's a young African American
girl who was shot and killed.

00:44:36.240 --> 00:44:37.660
She was in foster care.

00:44:37.660 --> 00:44:38.520
She had a knife,

00:44:38.520 --> 00:44:40.010
it looked in the body cam video

00:44:40.010 --> 00:44:42.520
like she was lunging at another girl.

00:44:42.520 --> 00:44:45.870
And initially, because
it was a Black girl,

00:44:45.870 --> 00:44:48.470
it was a child who was
killed by law enforcement,

00:44:48.470 --> 00:44:51.850
there was great alarm and the
body cam footage came out.

00:44:51.850 --> 00:44:53.197
People said, "Well, she had a knife.

00:44:53.197 --> 00:44:54.247
"It looked like she was lunging.

00:44:54.247 --> 00:44:56.667
"What else could the officer have done?

00:44:56.667 --> 00:44:57.727
"It was a good shooting.

00:44:57.727 --> 00:44:58.737
"It was a fine shooting.

00:44:58.737 --> 00:45:00.810
"How can you possibly object?"

00:45:00.810 --> 00:45:03.980
And I thought about that
between Brandon and Pastor Mike,

00:45:03.980 --> 00:45:07.003
because the conversation
we had at my center,

00:45:07.970 --> 00:45:12.970
woman named Tracy Lloyd
with tears in her eyes said,

00:45:13.087 --> 00:45:16.747
"We wouldn't have that conversation
as the only two options

00:45:16.747 --> 00:45:19.587
"like she was wronged, or she deserved it.

00:45:19.587 --> 00:45:23.067
"If the people who were in
charge of keeping us safe,

00:45:23.067 --> 00:45:24.107
"loved her."

00:45:27.490 --> 00:45:29.230
We would be able to imagine a world

00:45:29.230 --> 00:45:32.580
where a child who was moved
between multiple foster homes,

00:45:32.580 --> 00:45:35.760
which we know a right for
various kinds of abuse

00:45:35.760 --> 00:45:38.280
and neglect, right?

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:41.790
If that child's life was not
about what they chose to do

00:45:41.790 --> 00:45:46.163
in that moment, but the options
that were afforded to her.

00:45:47.730 --> 00:45:49.930
And perhaps interestingly,

00:45:49.930 --> 00:45:53.140
it paired in my mind with
the conversation I was having

00:45:53.140 --> 00:45:57.910
last night about the
tragic, tragic killing

00:45:57.910 --> 00:45:59.430
of a young woman,

00:45:59.430 --> 00:46:03.070
Christina Lee in Chinatown in New York.

00:46:03.070 --> 00:46:05.587
And it looks as if the
individual who stabbed her

00:46:05.587 --> 00:46:08.790
was a terrible murder stabbed her 40 times

00:46:08.790 --> 00:46:09.623
in her own apartment.

00:46:09.623 --> 00:46:12.450
It's a stranger issue,
which is incredibly rare

00:46:12.450 --> 00:46:14.450
as we know from the literature,

00:46:14.450 --> 00:46:18.090
just to the caricature of
the worst kinds of murder,

00:46:18.090 --> 00:46:21.710
but the individual who
stabbed her was 25 years old.

00:46:21.710 --> 00:46:24.203
Last address was a homeless shelter.

00:46:25.180 --> 00:46:26.240
And it was in a neighborhood

00:46:26.240 --> 00:46:28.550
where the community had
got banned together to say,

00:46:28.550 --> 00:46:30.210
we don't want more homeless shelters

00:46:30.210 --> 00:46:32.920
'cause we're scared of homeless people.

00:46:32.920 --> 00:46:36.540
And it says if because
the spectacle of violence

00:46:36.540 --> 00:46:40.470
is so extraordinary of
that physical violence,

00:46:40.470 --> 00:46:44.580
it's so extraordinary that
it blinds us to the violence

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:47.433
of poverty and neglect that
causes it in the first place.

00:46:49.000 --> 00:46:52.020
And so with the concerns around narrative,

00:46:52.020 --> 00:46:53.810
these concerns about what is missing,

00:46:53.810 --> 00:46:56.693
the levels that which we're
not having conversation.

00:46:57.810 --> 00:46:59.810
I have a question for the panel

00:46:59.810 --> 00:47:01.770
and I'm happy for y'all
to go in whatever order

00:47:01.770 --> 00:47:02.620
and to speak to each other,

00:47:02.620 --> 00:47:07.620
which is what obligation
is there among Black folks

00:47:09.500 --> 00:47:12.110
or folks who are championing
the Black radical imagination

00:47:12.110 --> 00:47:16.560
within this space to
the White assumptions,

00:47:16.560 --> 00:47:20.180
the White gaze upon this space?

00:47:20.180 --> 00:47:25.180
What things do Black folks owe
to what White people imagine

00:47:25.350 --> 00:47:27.630
around what's happening
in Black communities?

00:47:27.630 --> 00:47:30.700
'Cause it does seem as
if part of the narratives

00:47:30.700 --> 00:47:33.360
that are made invisible

00:47:33.360 --> 00:47:35.800
are the narratives that
are hardest to explain

00:47:35.800 --> 00:47:38.600
to a White imagination of Blackness.

00:47:38.600 --> 00:47:39.870
So if that question makes sense,

00:47:39.870 --> 00:47:41.910
I would love for any of
the panelists to go ahead

00:47:41.910 --> 00:47:42.743
and get started.

00:47:42.743 --> 00:47:43.610
And if nobody wants to,

00:47:43.610 --> 00:47:45.210
I absolutely will call on y'all.

00:47:50.180 --> 00:47:52.600
- I can start, my first response

00:47:52.600 --> 00:47:56.210
is we owe nothing to the White imagination

00:47:57.410 --> 00:47:59.840
because the White imagination
is not constructed

00:47:59.840 --> 00:48:00.960
And the way that we're talking about

00:48:00.960 --> 00:48:02.990
in the central bowl of
violence in that way,

00:48:02.990 --> 00:48:07.730
it's not constructed to
serve Black communities.

00:48:07.730 --> 00:48:10.150
And I think, you know,
each person laid that out,

00:48:10.150 --> 00:48:12.293
particularly, you know, Pastor Mike,

00:48:14.141 --> 00:48:19.141
this idea that the solution lies in there

00:48:19.780 --> 00:48:24.780
I think has been challenged
again and again and again.

00:48:24.910 --> 00:48:27.020
And I do see that as a
problem of the narrative.

00:48:27.020 --> 00:48:30.747
And so thinking about this,
I wrote an article recently,

00:48:30.747 --> 00:48:32.190
"I Am A Child," right?

00:48:32.190 --> 00:48:36.080
A girl child's truth and the
lie eyes of law enforcement.

00:48:36.080 --> 00:48:39.610
And it's about the girl child.

00:48:39.610 --> 00:48:42.410
And I'm using here the words
of June Jordan in Rochester,

00:48:42.410 --> 00:48:44.690
New York, who was pepper
sprayed by officers.

00:48:44.690 --> 00:48:45.523
And in that piece,

00:48:45.523 --> 00:48:49.170
I do a step by step
analysis of the encounter

00:48:49.170 --> 00:48:53.340
and the moment in which the
girl child tells the truth

00:48:53.340 --> 00:48:54.807
to law enforcement,

00:48:54.807 --> 00:48:59.740
"I Am A Child" calling them
on their failure to recognize

00:48:59.740 --> 00:49:02.160
to see them, but we understand,

00:49:02.160 --> 00:49:04.500
and your work is well,
Professor Goff, right?

00:49:04.500 --> 00:49:07.880
That these institutions
have been conditioned

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:09.770
to misread us, right?

00:49:09.770 --> 00:49:12.820
To not see us, right.

00:49:12.820 --> 00:49:15.600
And so you can imagine
that the work will go

00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:20.083
into either helping people
see or keeping us alive.

00:49:21.160 --> 00:49:23.018
And the work of keeping us alive is deep

00:49:23.018 --> 00:49:27.027
in the Black radical tradition, right?

00:49:27.027 --> 00:49:28.680
And in our daily practices.

00:49:28.680 --> 00:49:30.600
And if we think for
example about abolition

00:49:30.600 --> 00:49:34.350
and how that's emerged
as a possibility in a way

00:49:34.350 --> 00:49:37.230
in this moment that it
hasn't very long time.

00:49:37.230 --> 00:49:40.450
Part of that is recognizing
the day to day practices,

00:49:40.450 --> 00:49:42.540
not only abolition as a political project,

00:49:42.540 --> 00:49:44.170
but as a political project

00:49:44.170 --> 00:49:48.279
that's embedded in daily practices.

00:49:48.279 --> 00:49:52.060
That act of love that Pastor
Mike is talking about,

00:49:52.060 --> 00:49:56.870
I know a young person
is leading down a path

00:49:56.870 --> 00:49:58.430
and in my neighborhood,

00:49:58.430 --> 00:49:59.920
how do I approach them?

00:49:59.920 --> 00:50:02.660
Understanding the challenges with that,

00:50:02.660 --> 00:50:03.493
which is something,

00:50:03.493 --> 00:50:05.690
you know, I've read about
in the "Chosen Ones,"

00:50:05.690 --> 00:50:07.080
building that world.

00:50:07.080 --> 00:50:08.980
And one of the things that
I've learned from folks

00:50:08.980 --> 00:50:11.650
who are doing this work
over the last couple years,

00:50:11.650 --> 00:50:14.490
as we imagine the top down.

00:50:14.490 --> 00:50:17.180
At the top, people have moved
through this racial reckoning

00:50:17.180 --> 00:50:20.270
really the people on the ground
are still doing the work.

00:50:20.270 --> 00:50:21.220
Why are they doing the work?

00:50:21.220 --> 00:50:22.573
Because they're doing the care work

00:50:22.573 --> 00:50:24.930
that what it means to be a community,

00:50:24.930 --> 00:50:27.560
it's what it means to care for each other

00:50:27.560 --> 00:50:29.960
and people so people are doing that work

00:50:29.960 --> 00:50:32.210
and people are gonna
continue to do that work.

00:50:32.210 --> 00:50:35.090
I do think that thinking
about investing in that work

00:50:35.090 --> 00:50:36.963
is really important.

00:50:38.130 --> 00:50:40.380
The narrative for investment,
I think, you know,

00:50:40.380 --> 00:50:44.880
Pat's work is compelling
for folks because of that.

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:47.190
And at the same time,

00:50:47.190 --> 00:50:50.450
it has to be a narrative
that is centered in the value

00:50:50.450 --> 00:50:53.300
of Black life and Black humanity.

00:50:53.300 --> 00:50:56.093
And those two, right, can often collide.

00:50:57.727 --> 00:50:59.233
- And can I just add and say,

00:50:59.233 --> 00:51:03.420
it must be centered in Black
life and Black humanity,

00:51:03.420 --> 00:51:05.700
but also Black leadership

00:51:05.700 --> 00:51:09.860
or leadership of those
who demonstrate love,

00:51:09.860 --> 00:51:12.070
public love for Black people.

00:51:12.070 --> 00:51:14.740
And so I do find it quite problematic

00:51:14.740 --> 00:51:19.740
that we are allowing this
narrative around crime

00:51:22.570 --> 00:51:26.147
and violence in our
communities to just state

00:51:29.310 --> 00:51:31.680
or at least to go mainstream

00:51:31.680 --> 00:51:35.460
through the voices of some
Black institutional leaders,

00:51:35.460 --> 00:51:38.810
both political, religious,
cultural, and otherwise.

00:51:38.810 --> 00:51:40.410
You know, it's a
fascinating thing you know,

00:51:40.410 --> 00:51:45.410
a lot of the narrative around,
you know, the rise in crime

00:51:46.720 --> 00:51:50.053
and the need for kind
of punitive response.

00:51:50.950 --> 00:51:53.450
In the 90s, we know had
lots of faith leaders

00:51:53.450 --> 00:51:55.980
standing up in press
conferences with mayors

00:51:55.980 --> 00:51:58.000
and elected officials, you know,

00:51:58.000 --> 00:52:00.610
saying this is the way forward.

00:52:00.610 --> 00:52:04.010
And yet we also know that
over the last couple years,

00:52:04.010 --> 00:52:09.010
events like, what was
the name of this thing

00:52:09.070 --> 00:52:10.350
that the police were doing?

00:52:10.350 --> 00:52:14.444
It was called faith in the
blue or something like that.

00:52:14.444 --> 00:52:19.444
These targeted outreaches
to Black religious leaders

00:52:20.540 --> 00:52:24.350
to sit down with law enforcement
and craft public safety

00:52:24.350 --> 00:52:26.930
plan when the average faith leader

00:52:26.930 --> 00:52:29.220
is not even in relationships,

00:52:29.220 --> 00:52:32.717
with some of the very
impacted individuals.

00:52:32.717 --> 00:52:33.950
And I say, this doesn't favor

00:52:33.950 --> 00:52:35.470
because I had lots of clergy
calling me across the country,

00:52:35.470 --> 00:52:37.600
said, "Pastor Mike should
I get involved in this?"

00:52:37.600 --> 00:52:41.267
And I said, "If your first conversation

00:52:41.267 --> 00:52:43.897
"around violence is with a prosecutor,

00:52:43.897 --> 00:52:46.263
"a politician, or a police officer,

00:52:46.263 --> 00:52:48.727
""then you ought to
probably stay in the stands

00:52:48.727 --> 00:52:50.920
"til you talk to Pookie
or Ray-Ray," right?

00:52:50.920 --> 00:52:53.790
'Cause you don't have
enough information, right,

00:52:53.790 --> 00:52:55.250
to be in that conversation

00:52:55.250 --> 00:52:57.790
a way that actually will get the result

00:52:57.790 --> 00:52:58.960
that you say you want,

00:52:58.960 --> 00:53:00.490
which is peace in our communities.

00:53:00.490 --> 00:53:01.380
And so right now,

00:53:01.380 --> 00:53:03.103
as the Biden administration, right,

00:53:03.103 --> 00:53:07.280
we have been pushing out
this $5 billion investment

00:53:07.280 --> 00:53:09.990
that folks like in our fund,
peace coalition, other folks,

00:53:09.990 --> 00:53:13.300
you know, gave the information
architecture to construct,

00:53:13.300 --> 00:53:14.760
we're seeing right now across the country

00:53:14.760 --> 00:53:18.170
that mayors have received ARP funds

00:53:18.170 --> 00:53:19.690
over almost a billion dollars

00:53:19.690 --> 00:53:22.900
I think of mayor commitments
to put ARP funds,

00:53:22.900 --> 00:53:24.140
the American Rescue Plan dollars

00:53:24.140 --> 00:53:25.420
into community vice interventions,

00:53:25.420 --> 00:53:28.170
and less than 10% of those funds

00:53:28.170 --> 00:53:29.820
have even moved into the community.

00:53:29.820 --> 00:53:32.390
So it is not just even
about accessing resources

00:53:32.390 --> 00:53:34.760
is about having the leadership in place

00:53:34.760 --> 00:53:36.440
at the executive level,

00:53:36.440 --> 00:53:38.580
at the communal level
and in the neighborhoods

00:53:38.580 --> 00:53:42.527
that can show that their love
for our communities see them

00:53:42.527 --> 00:53:44.670
not just as a problem to be solved,

00:53:44.670 --> 00:53:46.370
but a solution to be unleashed.

00:53:46.370 --> 00:53:47.820
And that work,

00:53:47.820 --> 00:53:49.960
I think is really the next frontier

00:53:49.960 --> 00:53:52.240
of the Black radical tradition,

00:53:52.240 --> 00:53:53.950
not just from a scholastic point of view,

00:53:53.950 --> 00:53:57.480
but what does it mean to
radically love Black folks

00:53:57.480 --> 00:54:00.300
to be proximal to the
conditions of Black folks?

00:54:00.300 --> 00:54:03.390
What does it mean for intellectual giants

00:54:03.390 --> 00:54:07.240
in cities and universities to
be in proximity to organizers

00:54:07.240 --> 00:54:08.840
on the ground and folks in neighborhoods

00:54:08.840 --> 00:54:10.040
who are impacted by this

00:54:10.040 --> 00:54:12.290
so our work can be braided together?

00:54:12.290 --> 00:54:14.800
I think that is another
part of the conversation

00:54:14.800 --> 00:54:18.580
of how we collapse these
false distinctions and roles

00:54:18.580 --> 00:54:21.580
into something that could be
much more glorious and healing.

00:54:23.100 --> 00:54:24.780
- Thank you, go ahead, Brandon.

00:54:24.780 --> 00:54:29.780
- Yeah, I'm really
taking the endeavor part

00:54:30.250 --> 00:54:31.110
of this seriously.

00:54:31.110 --> 00:54:33.860
So I'm trying out ideas and I'm curious

00:54:33.860 --> 00:54:38.860
to get a response because
I think, you know,

00:54:39.990 --> 00:54:42.890
there's an amazing essay
by Frederick Douglass

00:54:42.890 --> 00:54:43.927
that I love to teach called,

00:54:43.927 --> 00:54:46.670
"Is it Right and Wise
to Kill a Kidnapper?"

00:54:46.670 --> 00:54:48.990
And he's talking about
fugitive slave catchers.

00:54:48.990 --> 00:54:50.220
And what I love about that essay

00:54:50.220 --> 00:54:52.340
is that it captures a real problem

00:54:52.340 --> 00:54:53.920
in Black political thought.

00:54:53.920 --> 00:54:56.840
Is that you're always thinking
about what are rights, right?

00:54:56.840 --> 00:55:01.100
What are duties, obligations,
what's legitimate?

00:55:01.100 --> 00:55:05.410
What do you have to do
as a matter of morality?

00:55:05.410 --> 00:55:06.950
And you're also trying to think

00:55:06.950 --> 00:55:10.150
about things would be good, right?

00:55:10.150 --> 00:55:12.260
So you might not be obligated to do them,

00:55:12.260 --> 00:55:15.650
but they would be good if
you, in fact, did do them

00:55:15.650 --> 00:55:17.470
and things that would be prudent.

00:55:17.470 --> 00:55:22.210
So things that might be wise
to do and may be neither good,

00:55:22.210 --> 00:55:27.210
nor just, and I go into
that digression just to say,

00:55:27.950 --> 00:55:32.950
I agree that we probably
have no obligation to dispel

00:55:34.810 --> 00:55:39.810
racist fantasies that proliferate
through the population

00:55:40.700 --> 00:55:44.473
or punitive stigmas
against the ghetto poor.

00:55:45.320 --> 00:55:48.240
But I think it is probably good to do so,

00:55:48.240 --> 00:55:50.483
and probably wise to do so,

00:55:51.610 --> 00:55:53.610
in so far as we think

00:55:54.520 --> 00:55:57.270
that there is no plausible go it alone,

00:55:57.270 --> 00:55:59.380
nationalist strategy.

00:55:59.380 --> 00:56:01.960
And again, it goes back to
part of what I was getting at

00:56:01.960 --> 00:56:04.100
in my remarks is that one of my concerns

00:56:04.100 --> 00:56:06.250
with the community violence paradigm

00:56:07.230 --> 00:56:10.550
is I think it does a lot
as an explanatory paradigm,

00:56:10.550 --> 00:56:13.710
'cause it's coming out of
sociology, community studies,

00:56:13.710 --> 00:56:15.610
social ecology,

00:56:15.610 --> 00:56:18.610
but it brings a bunch
of normative resonances

00:56:18.610 --> 00:56:20.460
from communitarianism,

00:56:20.460 --> 00:56:24.470
from nationalism about
the ethics of community.

00:56:24.470 --> 00:56:28.210
And the question is who's in
the community and who's not?

00:56:28.210 --> 00:56:31.167
If we respond to Pastor Mike who says,

00:56:31.167 --> 00:56:33.477
"Do we belong to America?"

00:56:34.620 --> 00:56:39.150
The right arguments might
not entail entrenching

00:56:39.150 --> 00:56:43.520
the problem deeper in an
isolated neighborhood space,

00:56:43.520 --> 00:56:45.870
they might be about tethering these things

00:56:45.870 --> 00:56:48.840
to a broader metropolitan strategy,

00:56:48.840 --> 00:56:52.030
a broader national strategy.

00:56:52.030 --> 00:56:55.000
That's one of my concerns here.

00:56:55.000 --> 00:56:58.730
And I'm just curious as to
how people respond to it

00:56:58.730 --> 00:57:02.820
because I think one of the
things that's really remarkable

00:57:02.820 --> 00:57:05.300
to me about out the last 10, 15 years

00:57:05.300 --> 00:57:09.700
is that all the hard
culture work, artistic work,

00:57:09.700 --> 00:57:14.700
hip hop, film, music, the hard
political work and activism

00:57:15.290 --> 00:57:18.700
I think has done a lot to
really change how people think

00:57:18.700 --> 00:57:22.390
about the agential nature of crime

00:57:22.390 --> 00:57:25.560
in ghetto community and the attribution

00:57:25.560 --> 00:57:27.880
of moral responsibility for it.

00:57:27.880 --> 00:57:29.930
And I think it's created
a broader constituency

00:57:29.930 --> 00:57:32.100
than we've ever seen for these movements,

00:57:32.100 --> 00:57:35.210
even though it's not
enough to win as of yet.

00:57:35.210 --> 00:57:39.363
So, I'm suspicious of the
reflects of worry about it.

00:57:40.527 --> 00:57:42.360
- You know, I appreciate that.

00:57:42.360 --> 00:57:46.410
And I would say that
it good to do the work,

00:57:46.410 --> 00:57:49.170
but it does not emerge
out of an obligation.

00:57:49.170 --> 00:57:50.003
- Right, I agree.

00:57:50.003 --> 00:57:53.070
- As an obligation suggests
criteria, you know,

00:57:53.070 --> 00:57:54.760
characteristics of a relationship

00:57:54.760 --> 00:57:58.920
that aren't actually their
relationship that we have, right.

00:57:58.920 --> 00:58:01.490
And the challenge that we're confronting

00:58:01.490 --> 00:58:02.723
and the language and the narrative,

00:58:02.723 --> 00:58:04.290
I think is really important.

00:58:04.290 --> 00:58:06.570
We are talking about evolution of a system

00:58:06.570 --> 00:58:09.840
of racial domination in which
law enforcement has played

00:58:09.840 --> 00:58:13.020
a central role, both
formal and informal, right.

00:58:13.020 --> 00:58:16.900
Intra-legal, extra-legal
forms of law enforcement.

00:58:16.900 --> 00:58:19.930
And that's what we are
contending with, right.

00:58:19.930 --> 00:58:23.170
And a body politic that has supported that

00:58:23.170 --> 00:58:25.590
in its different iterations over time.

00:58:25.590 --> 00:58:27.580
And so we do the work

00:58:27.580 --> 00:58:30.890
because the same reasons
that Du Bois did the work

00:58:30.890 --> 00:58:32.297
and Ida B. Wells did the work, right.

00:58:32.297 --> 00:58:33.130
And on and on,

00:58:33.130 --> 00:58:34.320
and we do the work

00:58:34.320 --> 00:58:37.134
and we have the
conversations within, right.

00:58:37.134 --> 00:58:40.000
That aren't directed toward the White gays

00:58:40.000 --> 00:58:41.900
and move us forward.

00:58:41.900 --> 00:58:44.300
But I wouldn't say I
would stop short of saying

00:58:44.300 --> 00:58:49.300
it's an obligation, it really
is about saving our own lives.

00:58:51.180 --> 00:58:54.630
And I don't say that in a
hyperbolic sense at all.

00:58:54.630 --> 00:58:56.110
I mean, I know you appreciate that,

00:58:56.110 --> 00:58:58.210
but when you look at
the historical record,

00:58:58.210 --> 00:59:00.040
that's what folks are trying to do.

00:59:00.040 --> 00:59:01.530
And somehow we imagine that,

00:59:01.530 --> 00:59:03.030
that we are in a different moment

00:59:03.030 --> 00:59:05.650
and yet that is still
what we're trying to do.

00:59:05.650 --> 00:59:06.483
Right.

00:59:07.490 --> 00:59:08.323
- Yeah.

00:59:08.323 --> 00:59:09.500
- Could I react to?

00:59:09.500 --> 00:59:11.290
- Go ahead, go ahead Pastor Mike.

00:59:11.290 --> 00:59:12.920
- Just real quick, I wanted
to react to another part

00:59:12.920 --> 00:59:13.753
of what Brandon said,

00:59:13.753 --> 00:59:17.590
because, you know, I do find
the language of community

00:59:17.590 --> 00:59:22.590
violence as a kind of
descriptor of the way

00:59:23.840 --> 00:59:25.410
in which violence, uniquely visits,

00:59:25.410 --> 00:59:26.480
Black and Brown communities

00:59:26.480 --> 00:59:29.930
within the kind of larger
context of the gun violence

00:59:29.930 --> 00:59:33.240
conversation in this
country to be an output

00:59:33.240 --> 00:59:37.440
of how many of us who have been
working at the intersection

00:59:37.440 --> 00:59:41.460
of gun violence, victimization,
Black and Brown communities

00:59:41.460 --> 00:59:45.410
largely found our
experiences being erased,

00:59:45.410 --> 00:59:49.900
if not altogether left to
a law enforcement punitive

00:59:49.900 --> 00:59:51.490
only conversation.

00:59:51.490 --> 00:59:54.300
So as to when you join a gun violence

00:59:54.300 --> 00:59:56.100
prevention conversation,

00:59:56.100 --> 00:59:58.510
and the only conversation
about second amendment,

00:59:58.510 --> 01:00:00.000
right? Right?

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:01.630
And then folks are like, well,

01:00:01.630 --> 01:00:03.940
what about these other public
health related strategy?

01:00:03.940 --> 01:00:06.410
What about talking about
criminal justice reform

01:00:06.410 --> 01:00:09.230
within the "gun violence"
prevention movement,

01:00:09.230 --> 01:00:12.250
or talking about issues of poverty

01:00:12.250 --> 01:00:14.110
and other forms of violence?

01:00:14.110 --> 01:00:18.130
We were told, well, that is
not gun violence prevention.

01:00:18.130 --> 01:00:18.963
- [Philip] Yeah.
- Work.

01:00:18.963 --> 01:00:20.720
Right, and so we had
to create new language.

01:00:20.720 --> 01:00:21.553
And I just say all that,

01:00:21.553 --> 01:00:25.990
it's say part of what I
think could be an endeavor

01:00:25.990 --> 01:00:30.150
is how do we create new language together

01:00:31.130 --> 01:00:32.700
that actually captures that,

01:00:32.700 --> 01:00:36.390
which we say we are trying to accomplish.

01:00:36.390 --> 01:00:39.930
And I do think this is
perhaps part of the project

01:00:39.930 --> 01:00:43.010
of building and reimagining public safety

01:00:43.010 --> 01:00:47.110
by centering those directly
impacted by these issues

01:00:47.110 --> 01:00:51.080
versus us doing a reactionary move,

01:00:51.080 --> 01:00:54.970
whether it's around narrative
policy and or solutions

01:00:54.970 --> 01:00:58.070
to these arbitrary rises and spikes

01:00:58.070 --> 01:01:01.690
in crime and violence
that are often politicized

01:01:01.690 --> 01:01:06.000
in the moment but not necessarily
over a long period of time

01:01:06.000 --> 01:01:09.370
done as a constructive
project around democracy,

01:01:09.370 --> 01:01:10.240
because, you know,

01:01:10.240 --> 01:01:13.020
how can one, you know,

01:01:13.020 --> 01:01:16.590
really love the country
or the city they're in,

01:01:16.590 --> 01:01:21.330
if they can't even imagine
they're going to live very long

01:01:21.330 --> 01:01:22.390
within it, right?

01:01:22.390 --> 01:01:26.330
It is almost a decoupling
of one spirit in humanity.

01:01:26.330 --> 01:01:29.036
And so I do think the
community violence narrative

01:01:29.036 --> 01:01:32.490
is both helpful, but also challenging.

01:01:32.490 --> 01:01:35.620
And I hope we can endeavor to, again,

01:01:35.620 --> 01:01:37.860
be very clear about what we want to say

01:01:37.860 --> 01:01:40.140
and how we want to
imagine the world forward

01:01:40.140 --> 01:01:42.040
when we're talking about these issues.

01:01:43.890 --> 01:01:44.930
- I appreciate all that.

01:01:44.930 --> 01:01:46.090
And Brandon I wanna add too,

01:01:46.090 --> 01:01:47.920
and then pivot to a different question,

01:01:47.920 --> 01:01:50.390
which is a synthesis of
a couple that I've gotten

01:01:50.390 --> 01:01:51.363
in the chat here.

01:01:52.370 --> 01:01:54.380
'Cause I think part of
what you're bringing up

01:01:54.380 --> 01:01:57.400
is that regardless of what
a moral obligation might be,

01:01:57.400 --> 01:02:02.400
particularly from a collective
and community perspective,

01:02:02.770 --> 01:02:05.930
there is work that is likely necessary

01:02:05.930 --> 01:02:07.960
in order to achieve certain names.

01:02:07.960 --> 01:02:12.960
This group is not called
Black elected political work

01:02:14.160 --> 01:02:16.430
in the context of community violence.

01:02:16.430 --> 01:02:20.090
It is called right, Black activism

01:02:20.090 --> 01:02:22.520
and so I'm wondering to the
degree to which, and Pat,

01:02:22.520 --> 01:02:25.600
if I can sort of direct this to you,

01:02:25.600 --> 01:02:28.000
the degree to which it is clear

01:02:28.000 --> 01:02:30.140
what the role of activism should be

01:02:30.140 --> 01:02:33.210
versus what the role of the
state should be in this,

01:02:33.210 --> 01:02:34.500
particularly at a moment

01:02:34.500 --> 01:02:36.390
when we're seeing the federal government

01:02:36.390 --> 01:02:38.150
is putting more money into programs

01:02:38.150 --> 01:02:40.118
like violence interruption, right?

01:02:40.118 --> 01:02:42.510
And that federal and and state dollars

01:02:42.510 --> 01:02:45.550
are going more to these
nonprofit community elements.

01:02:45.550 --> 01:02:48.080
The nonprofit sphere has
traditionally been supposed

01:02:48.080 --> 01:02:51.490
to be for places that the
state need not or cannot go,

01:02:51.490 --> 01:02:53.440
and yet, as we think
about scaling responses

01:02:53.440 --> 01:02:54.730
to community violence,

01:02:54.730 --> 01:02:57.090
we see the state starting to get involved,

01:02:57.090 --> 01:02:59.990
which leads to this sort of muddying

01:02:59.990 --> 01:03:02.800
where there are folks who are
saying no defund the police

01:03:02.800 --> 01:03:05.840
as the slogan needs to serve
these particular partisan

01:03:05.840 --> 01:03:08.300
interests versus it has
nothing to do with y'all.

01:03:08.300 --> 01:03:09.133
Please don't talk about it

01:03:09.133 --> 01:03:10.329
'cause you didn't show up at the rally.

01:03:10.329 --> 01:03:12.620
So you don't get a say.

01:03:12.620 --> 01:03:16.160
So Pat, can you talk just a
little bit about this moment

01:03:16.160 --> 01:03:20.230
that we've got where there is
funding flowing to nonprofits

01:03:20.230 --> 01:03:23.300
from the state and whether
or not some of the work

01:03:23.300 --> 01:03:24.730
that nonprofits have been doing,

01:03:24.730 --> 01:03:25.900
particularly the work you've been studying

01:03:25.900 --> 01:03:29.833
belongs with the state or does
it belong with the community?

01:03:31.690 --> 01:03:32.790
- Yeah, big questions.

01:03:33.840 --> 01:03:37.750
You know, I think of this
topic that we're talking about

01:03:37.750 --> 01:03:39.680
was created by the state.

01:03:39.680 --> 01:03:44.410
This was a result of interventions

01:03:44.410 --> 01:03:47.310
over a long period of time.

01:03:47.310 --> 01:03:50.470
And I think one of the points that I heard

01:03:50.470 --> 01:03:54.220
Dr. Terry push us to maybe think about

01:03:54.220 --> 01:03:59.090
is how do we talk about
this problem of violence

01:03:59.090 --> 01:04:01.840
and expand it so that it
we're talking about it

01:04:01.840 --> 01:04:03.410
in relational terms?

01:04:03.410 --> 01:04:06.730
We're talking about it, not as a problem

01:04:06.730 --> 01:04:10.640
that is concentrated in low
income communities of color,

01:04:10.640 --> 01:04:14.800
but as a consequence of
a set of interventions

01:04:14.800 --> 01:04:17.240
that have been carried out over decades

01:04:17.240 --> 01:04:20.800
and longer, where, and
I saw Elizabeth Hinton

01:04:20.800 --> 01:04:22.880
is in the room somewhere.

01:04:22.880 --> 01:04:25.860
She should, everything that
I've learned about this,

01:04:25.860 --> 01:04:29.610
I've learned from reading her
work and other historians,

01:04:29.610 --> 01:04:32.770
but this is a product,

01:04:32.770 --> 01:04:34.650
this is actively produced

01:04:34.650 --> 01:04:36.850
when the federal government invested

01:04:36.850 --> 01:04:39.740
in an interstate highway
system and subsidized mortgages

01:04:39.740 --> 01:04:42.750
that allowed people who could afford a car

01:04:42.750 --> 01:04:47.750
and firms were able to
lead central city unions,

01:04:47.840 --> 01:04:49.930
central cities to avoid big city unions

01:04:49.930 --> 01:04:52.650
that have become more and more powerful

01:04:52.650 --> 01:04:55.850
when we give enormous capacity

01:04:55.850 --> 01:04:59.740
to local municipal governments
to decide who can live

01:04:59.740 --> 01:05:01.430
in that space.

01:05:01.430 --> 01:05:03.360
So if you like, for instance,

01:05:03.360 --> 01:05:05.740
if you wanna look at
St. Louis in Ferguson,

01:05:05.740 --> 01:05:10.280
well first think back to
when the White population

01:05:10.280 --> 01:05:13.240
of St. Louis was subsidized
to lead to the suburbs

01:05:13.240 --> 01:05:15.980
and then formed new municipalities

01:05:15.980 --> 01:05:19.280
and then zoned it all
for single family homes

01:05:19.280 --> 01:05:22.450
so that their property
values rose really quickly,

01:05:22.450 --> 01:05:25.880
and the property values in
St. Louis fell very quickly.

01:05:25.880 --> 01:05:29.770
That's the basis for how
you get decades later

01:05:29.770 --> 01:05:31.140
what happened to Michael Brown?

01:05:31.140 --> 01:05:34.480
That's the basis for how St.
Louis became the most violent

01:05:34.480 --> 01:05:35.680
city in the country.

01:05:35.680 --> 01:05:37.610
Those active of interventions,

01:05:37.610 --> 01:05:40.880
which now take the form
of land use regulations

01:05:40.880 --> 01:05:45.880
and gated communities and
this spatial infrastructure

01:05:45.940 --> 01:05:50.570
of inequality that the
state has built over time.

01:05:50.570 --> 01:05:53.330
So when I'm listening to Dr. Terry,

01:05:53.330 --> 01:05:58.330
in my mind, it reinforced this notion

01:05:58.360 --> 01:06:00.970
that we have to understand this challenge

01:06:00.970 --> 01:06:02.070
in relational terms,

01:06:02.070 --> 01:06:05.480
we have to understand it as
a consequence of conscious,

01:06:05.480 --> 01:06:09.570
intentional, interventions
carried out over decades

01:06:09.570 --> 01:06:13.230
that have led to this form
of extreme inequality,

01:06:13.230 --> 01:06:16.150
which then creates the
conditions for vials to emerge.

01:06:16.150 --> 01:06:18.120
When people retreat from their homes,

01:06:18.120 --> 01:06:20.537
when they've the core
institutions of the community

01:06:20.537 --> 01:06:22.130
are not supported,

01:06:22.130 --> 01:06:26.010
then you create the conditions
where violence can emerge.

01:06:26.010 --> 01:06:30.613
So circling back to your question, Phil,

01:06:31.800 --> 01:06:34.810
I think it is unfair.

01:06:34.810 --> 01:06:36.770
I think it is unsustainable.

01:06:36.770 --> 01:06:41.095
And I think it is in effective
to think of this project

01:06:41.095 --> 01:06:45.310
of responding to these
interventions carried over

01:06:45.310 --> 01:06:49.490
out over decades with another project

01:06:49.490 --> 01:06:51.830
that doesn't feature the
state with another project

01:06:51.830 --> 01:06:55.350
that doesn't assume that
we need public funding,

01:06:55.350 --> 01:06:59.080
120 billion a year spent
on law enforcement.

01:06:59.080 --> 01:07:02.530
You know, what if the
groups run by Pastor Mike

01:07:02.530 --> 01:07:07.360
and Fund Peace had a 100
billion dollar budget per year,

01:07:07.360 --> 01:07:09.950
per year, not 5 billion
at one point in time,

01:07:09.950 --> 01:07:12.450
but a 100 billion per year.

01:07:12.450 --> 01:07:16.590
What would communities
look like at that point?

01:07:16.590 --> 01:07:18.070
And so, you know, I always go back

01:07:18.070 --> 01:07:23.070
when I lived to in Manhattan for 11 years,

01:07:23.130 --> 01:07:27.730
and I was in NYU housing
right in Washington Square.

01:07:27.730 --> 01:07:32.730
And I was not once asked to
volunteer my time or my money

01:07:34.860 --> 01:07:38.699
to go deal with the problem
of violence in my community.

01:07:38.699 --> 01:07:43.440
And so if we're going to
mount a sustained project

01:07:43.440 --> 01:07:44.273
to deal with this,

01:07:44.273 --> 01:07:46.900
I don't think it can rely
on people volunteering.

01:07:46.900 --> 01:07:49.230
I don't think it can rely on organizations

01:07:49.230 --> 01:07:52.150
that are fighting for
every dollar they receive.

01:07:52.150 --> 01:07:54.700
I think it has to be a
project that is funded

01:07:54.700 --> 01:07:56.450
through the mechanism of the state.

01:07:58.298 --> 01:08:02.680
- And I'll just add to that,
that as much as I appreciate

01:08:02.680 --> 01:08:04.689
Pastor Mike and the work that he does

01:08:04.689 --> 01:08:07.090
and the work that folks
I've written about are doing

01:08:07.090 --> 01:08:09.130
as well there's a way
that that can presume

01:08:09.130 --> 01:08:10.730
that the conditions don't change

01:08:10.730 --> 01:08:12.760
so that the organizations
are going to be needed

01:08:12.760 --> 01:08:15.740
to do the work that they're doing now.

01:08:15.740 --> 01:08:17.030
So if we take a step back,

01:08:17.030 --> 01:08:18.510
we're actually talking about a project

01:08:18.510 --> 01:08:19.900
of reparations, right.

01:08:19.900 --> 01:08:22.250
An investment, right.

01:08:22.250 --> 01:08:26.030
An acknowledgement of the harm
and some financial redress

01:08:26.030 --> 01:08:28.502
for all of what the state interventions

01:08:28.502 --> 01:08:31.100
that Pat just laid up.

01:08:31.100 --> 01:08:33.230
But we stopped short of that, right.

01:08:33.230 --> 01:08:36.377
And there's a reason that
we stop short of that,

01:08:36.377 --> 01:08:39.490
and our stopping short of that
is what allows the problem

01:08:39.490 --> 01:08:42.630
to continue and us to
have these conversations

01:08:42.630 --> 01:08:45.250
about which kinds of interventions

01:08:45.250 --> 01:08:46.860
make the most sense, right.

01:08:46.860 --> 01:08:49.060
And what we ought to do,

01:08:49.060 --> 01:08:52.700
not acknowledging that we are
not all the same we, right.

01:08:52.700 --> 01:08:55.060
And the community is not all
the same community, right.

01:08:55.060 --> 01:08:57.550
The politics of the community are real.

01:08:57.550 --> 01:09:00.360
And those are also
manufactured both internally,

01:09:00.360 --> 01:09:01.440
but also by the state.

01:09:01.440 --> 01:09:04.700
So I think part of what I've tried to do

01:09:04.700 --> 01:09:07.630
over the last couple of years is to think

01:09:07.630 --> 01:09:10.790
in a real, you know, honest
way about the situation

01:09:10.790 --> 01:09:12.977
in which we find ourselves, right.

01:09:12.977 --> 01:09:16.510
And if we think about what Pat laid out

01:09:16.510 --> 01:09:19.510
around residential segregation
and the construction

01:09:19.510 --> 01:09:24.510
of Whiteness and the battle
for Whiteness right now.

01:09:25.310 --> 01:09:27.570
And what we saw in the insurrection

01:09:27.570 --> 01:09:29.040
is the kind of crisis of Whiteness.

01:09:29.040 --> 01:09:32.120
Like what is Whiteness
gonna mean in this country?

01:09:32.120 --> 01:09:33.790
And that's consequential for Black people,

01:09:33.790 --> 01:09:35.740
to the extent we're talking
about Black people, right.

01:09:35.740 --> 01:09:38.497
Because of always include
some reference to Black people

01:09:38.497 --> 01:09:40.370
and the condition of Black people,

01:09:40.370 --> 01:09:45.370
whether they are there or
not, right in this nation.

01:09:45.610 --> 01:09:48.450
So the question isn't
as much about, you know,

01:09:48.450 --> 01:09:50.760
are we part of America, right?

01:09:50.760 --> 01:09:53.740
It is, is America an America

01:09:53.740 --> 01:09:57.343
that has the kinds of
institutions that can include us?

01:09:58.690 --> 01:10:02.330
So what I've done there is to
take us like many degrees away

01:10:02.330 --> 01:10:06.190
from what people, the narrative
of gun violence, right.

01:10:06.190 --> 01:10:08.100
And building on what
other folks have said.

01:10:08.100 --> 01:10:11.140
And, you know, I say
that not to stop put halt

01:10:11.140 --> 01:10:14.540
to the conversation, but
just to kind of, you know,

01:10:14.540 --> 01:10:17.820
to really get us closer to the problem

01:10:17.820 --> 01:10:22.423
as we're dealing with it as
it as we're confronting it.

01:10:24.060 --> 01:10:25.600
- Yeah, and Brandon, I see
you take yourself off mute.

01:10:25.600 --> 01:10:26.990
You wanna get in on this?

01:10:26.990 --> 01:10:28.620
- Yeah.

01:10:28.620 --> 01:10:31.863
Because I think to this point,

01:10:34.116 --> 01:10:36.630
you know, we're thinking in the aftermath

01:10:36.630 --> 01:10:37.950
of some really momentous things,

01:10:37.950 --> 01:10:41.023
and I really appreciate it
how you set us up, Phil.

01:10:42.330 --> 01:10:47.330
It's not just the biggest protest
movement in world history.

01:10:48.200 --> 01:10:51.240
It's also the largest influx of cash

01:10:52.090 --> 01:10:56.593
into Black led political
organizations in history.

01:10:58.030 --> 01:11:01.950
And I think one of the things
that many of us knew before

01:11:01.950 --> 01:11:05.120
last month, but have all been
forced to deal with in public

01:11:05.120 --> 01:11:07.853
last month is that that didn't go well.

01:11:11.070 --> 01:11:13.970
Cash came in, there was an
infrastructure to deal with it.

01:11:15.120 --> 01:11:18.540
It papered over a lot of
rhetoric papered over,

01:11:18.540 --> 01:11:21.250
who was making decisions,

01:11:21.250 --> 01:11:23.693
what constituencies they
were accountable to,

01:11:24.940 --> 01:11:28.910
what philanthropic
dollars demanded in return

01:11:28.910 --> 01:11:30.723
for their commitments.

01:11:32.150 --> 01:11:35.540
That it's a whole set
of democratic deficits

01:11:35.540 --> 01:11:40.540
that emerge when this
happens in an ad hoc fashion,

01:11:42.330 --> 01:11:47.330
as a dance between philanthropists,
activist and a media

01:11:47.740 --> 01:11:51.930
climate that gravitates
towards spectacular acts

01:11:51.930 --> 01:11:52.823
of violence.

01:11:54.320 --> 01:11:55.783
So I just raised that to say

01:11:55.783 --> 01:11:59.000
that one of the biggest
problems for Black activism,

01:11:59.000 --> 01:12:03.630
one, which we have basically
no conceptual resources

01:12:03.630 --> 01:12:06.640
to deal with, 'cause it
hasn't been a problem before.

01:12:06.640 --> 01:12:10.290
It's like how to manage
enormous amounts of money

01:12:11.240 --> 01:12:15.680
in a way that is
democratically defensible.

01:12:15.680 --> 01:12:19.720
Or if not, it's not going to
appeal to norms of democracy,

01:12:19.720 --> 01:12:22.340
give us reasons why we should not demand

01:12:22.340 --> 01:12:25.853
those democratic norms
be shown fidelity to.

01:12:28.636 --> 01:12:33.636
The alternative, what I think
Professor Sharkey's laying out

01:12:34.130 --> 01:12:36.223
is really a remaking of the state.

01:12:37.070 --> 01:12:39.440
And again, that's just not a project

01:12:39.440 --> 01:12:42.120
that Black political thought has tended

01:12:42.120 --> 01:12:45.120
to have a lot of
resources to try to think,

01:12:45.120 --> 01:12:48.060
but it's our opportunity to do so,

01:12:48.060 --> 01:12:50.900
to establish a new progressivism

01:12:50.900 --> 01:12:54.930
that really thinks about what
might new and novel state

01:12:54.930 --> 01:12:57.080
institutions look like

01:12:57.080 --> 01:13:02.080
as we try to onboard the
work of community repair

01:13:02.100 --> 01:13:06.150
into a state apparatus
that might be deserving

01:13:06.150 --> 01:13:09.450
of the ascent and respect and commitment

01:13:09.450 --> 01:13:12.673
of the people who are right
now the truly disadvantaged.

01:13:14.513 --> 01:13:16.440
- Yeah Brandon, I just wanna
pick up on one thread of that.

01:13:16.440 --> 01:13:19.380
I'm so glad that you were
talking about the amount of money

01:13:19.380 --> 01:13:22.810
that philanthropy puts in
to Black Lego organizations,

01:13:22.810 --> 01:13:24.010
Black led political power,

01:13:24.010 --> 01:13:26.280
but it has been to the
criminal legal space

01:13:26.280 --> 01:13:28.600
and to the public safety
space in particular

01:13:28.600 --> 01:13:30.360
over the last two years.

01:13:30.360 --> 01:13:34.070
I wanna just sort of add
friendly amendments on two parts.

01:13:34.070 --> 01:13:36.870
One while the public rhetoric

01:13:36.870 --> 01:13:39.340
was about Black led organizations
and the public scrutiny

01:13:39.340 --> 01:13:41.650
has absolutely been
Black led organizations.

01:13:41.650 --> 01:13:42.900
The majority of the money

01:13:42.900 --> 01:13:44.020
that went into the public safety space

01:13:44.020 --> 01:13:48.050
was not to Black led organizations, right?

01:13:48.050 --> 01:13:53.050
And this sort of new
problem of sort of windfall

01:13:53.480 --> 01:13:56.300
of capital within the
space is absolutely new

01:13:56.300 --> 01:13:57.800
to Black political projects,

01:13:57.800 --> 01:14:02.050
but not new to nonprofit
spaces quite nonprofits, right?

01:14:02.050 --> 01:14:05.260
So there are examples of this
happening within the climate

01:14:05.260 --> 01:14:09.010
justice space, within women's
reproductive health space,

01:14:09.010 --> 01:14:11.130
with sexual orientation spaces

01:14:11.130 --> 01:14:16.130
where exactly the same kinds
of sort of cardboard edifices

01:14:16.280 --> 01:14:19.640
on the fronts of nonprofits fall down

01:14:19.640 --> 01:14:21.600
with the windfall of cash, right?

01:14:21.600 --> 01:14:24.400
And then the ecosystem
sort of reconstructs itself

01:14:24.400 --> 01:14:27.230
amidst that this is a predictable cycle

01:14:27.230 --> 01:14:29.300
when we rely on nongovernmental.

01:14:29.300 --> 01:14:30.620
So it's philanthropic,

01:14:30.620 --> 01:14:32.880
non-governmental resources
to get stuff done.

01:14:32.880 --> 01:14:34.420
And yet I do believe that the narrative

01:14:34.420 --> 01:14:37.040
that's gonna emerge from this
is gonna be quite distinct

01:14:37.040 --> 01:14:40.323
because of the racialization
of this work, right?

01:14:41.370 --> 01:14:45.350
Oh boy, do I wish that we had
seven hours to do just this.

01:14:45.350 --> 01:14:47.570
I wanna be responsive to all the messages

01:14:47.570 --> 01:14:49.064
that are coming in.

01:14:49.064 --> 01:14:51.240
It looks like 100% of
them are direct messages.

01:14:51.240 --> 01:14:52.830
Right now so I wanna make a pivot

01:14:52.830 --> 01:14:54.440
'cause I've had two folks ask now

01:14:54.440 --> 01:14:59.420
about given that we're
talking about attention

01:14:59.420 --> 01:15:02.530
between whether or not we want
community to genuinely lead

01:15:02.530 --> 01:15:05.670
outside of government where
we wanna be remaking the state

01:15:05.670 --> 01:15:07.033
as Brandon, you framed,

01:15:08.060 --> 01:15:12.460
this question as to what
is the role of activism

01:15:12.460 --> 01:15:15.650
if it is genuinely community
or to be community led

01:15:15.650 --> 01:15:20.130
in responding to community trauma per se,

01:15:20.130 --> 01:15:24.230
as opposed to medical
community and the state,

01:15:24.230 --> 01:15:27.700
considering the historic
neglect of community trauma

01:15:27.700 --> 01:15:29.770
particularly in Black communities,

01:15:29.770 --> 01:15:30.750
and Pastor Mark I'm gonna go ahead

01:15:30.750 --> 01:15:32.393
and give that to you first.

01:15:34.390 --> 01:15:36.560
- Well, I think the role is the both end

01:15:36.560 --> 01:15:38.730
but the emphasis to me, you know,

01:15:38.730 --> 01:15:40.860
continues to have to be around, you know,

01:15:40.860 --> 01:15:42.423
both leadership and agencies.

01:15:43.280 --> 01:15:45.387
You know, I think many
of us who are in that

01:15:45.387 --> 01:15:47.860
"activism organizing space,"

01:15:47.860 --> 01:15:51.270
appreciate that every system we engage

01:15:51.270 --> 01:15:56.030
is one built inherently with
Anti-Blackness embedded in it.

01:15:56.030 --> 01:15:58.150
Like none of us are
like walking around here

01:15:58.150 --> 01:16:03.140
really thinking that whether
it's a Black, Brown, Latino,

01:16:03.140 --> 01:16:08.100
Latin, that and that, LGBTQ
person leading such institution

01:16:08.100 --> 01:16:10.773
anti-Blackness is all through that.

01:16:11.920 --> 01:16:16.920
And yet we know that we must
figure out ways to redeem

01:16:17.640 --> 01:16:21.893
what's redeemable and transform
what must be jettison.

01:16:23.440 --> 01:16:25.780
And so for us, it's
about imagination, right?

01:16:25.780 --> 01:16:30.410
It's about how can we
create new structures

01:16:30.410 --> 01:16:33.650
within the redeemable
parts of these systems

01:16:34.900 --> 01:16:38.260
that have community, power, leadership,

01:16:38.260 --> 01:16:41.670
and accountability at the center of it,

01:16:41.670 --> 01:16:43.960
not in some kind of ceremonial way,

01:16:43.960 --> 01:16:46.360
but in a real distinct way,

01:16:46.360 --> 01:16:48.160
we can fire, we can hire,

01:16:48.160 --> 01:16:53.160
we can audit, we can make
corrective kind of decision making

01:16:53.350 --> 01:16:58.350
along the way to ensure
that community activism,

01:16:59.850 --> 01:17:04.850
interest, priorities remain
at the heart of these said

01:17:05.170 --> 01:17:09.180
both interventions or kind
of programmatic responses

01:17:09.180 --> 01:17:12.630
to both the immediacy of the problem

01:17:12.630 --> 01:17:15.610
and certainly the kind
of long term trajectory

01:17:15.610 --> 01:17:16.530
of the solution.

01:17:16.530 --> 01:17:18.650
One thing we ask often
and we wrestle with this

01:17:18.650 --> 01:17:20.870
in our organizing is I love
to put this out for folks

01:17:20.870 --> 01:17:25.870
here is, you know, what is
the trajectory, the timeline,

01:17:26.870 --> 01:17:29.270
the kind of Hansel and Gretel crumbs,

01:17:29.270 --> 01:17:31.700
Hansel and Gretel crumbs that you put

01:17:31.700 --> 01:17:35.380
so people can follow along in the journey

01:17:35.380 --> 01:17:37.617
towards abolitionist reality

01:17:40.669 --> 01:17:45.669
or a reality where police budgets
are less than the majority

01:17:48.210 --> 01:17:51.070
or significant part of a general fund

01:17:51.070 --> 01:17:55.250
and we are as Brother Sharkey
you mentioned literally

01:17:55.250 --> 01:17:58.470
that dollar for dollar investing
in both community healing,

01:17:58.470 --> 01:18:01.690
community repair, repressions, like,

01:18:01.690 --> 01:18:03.420
you know, what's the timeline to that?

01:18:03.420 --> 01:18:04.310
How do we get there?

01:18:04.310 --> 01:18:06.570
How do we help people appreciate that

01:18:06.570 --> 01:18:08.220
organizing along the way,

01:18:08.220 --> 01:18:11.430
activism along the way is
the trajectory of that?

01:18:11.430 --> 01:18:14.140
And it's very hard when the
material conditions of people

01:18:14.140 --> 01:18:19.140
every day are experiencing
the concreteness

01:18:19.300 --> 01:18:20.650
of the loss of lives,

01:18:20.650 --> 01:18:25.650
the bruising of bodies, the
intimate partner violence,

01:18:25.706 --> 01:18:30.020
the lethal conflicts that play out,

01:18:30.020 --> 01:18:31.870
literally outside people's homes.

01:18:31.870 --> 01:18:34.430
Like folks can't think
20 years in the future,

01:18:34.430 --> 01:18:35.990
if they can't imagine how

01:18:35.990 --> 01:18:38.900
to get their kid to school today, right.

01:18:38.900 --> 01:18:42.560
And so our task is to
help balance the tension

01:18:42.560 --> 01:18:45.950
between the hearing now and
the Black future, right.

01:18:45.950 --> 01:18:47.859
I hear folks talk about Black history.

01:18:47.859 --> 01:18:48.710
You know, when you talk
about Black future,

01:18:48.710 --> 01:18:50.462
I say, no, we talking about all of it,

01:18:50.462 --> 01:18:55.462
but not one sector of our work
can hold all of it, right?

01:18:55.540 --> 01:18:59.200
And so this is where I think
it has to be a both end

01:18:59.200 --> 01:19:04.200
in my mind, both around
imagining, around implementing,

01:19:05.840 --> 01:19:09.330
around evaluating, around sustaining.

01:19:09.330 --> 01:19:12.630
And some of that will be
done in some of these systems

01:19:12.630 --> 01:19:15.826
that have both redemptive and
unredemptive components of it.

01:19:15.826 --> 01:19:18.320
And some will have to be done
apart from those systems.

01:19:18.320 --> 01:19:21.040
The question is, can our
brilliance, our humility,

01:19:21.040 --> 01:19:24.360
our collaboration, our
intentionality help us

01:19:24.360 --> 01:19:26.890
be able to change both the near term

01:19:26.890 --> 01:19:28.550
and the far term conditions?

01:19:28.550 --> 01:19:30.720
And I think that is a big part of work,

01:19:30.720 --> 01:19:34.530
but I am always captured by this question,

01:19:34.530 --> 01:19:36.160
Dr. King, "How Long?" Right.

01:19:36.160 --> 01:19:38.620
And I think that is a question
for so many in the community,

01:19:38.620 --> 01:19:40.670
how long must we live in these conditions

01:19:42.020 --> 01:19:45.123
without reprieve and reparations,

01:19:47.448 --> 01:19:48.940
a lot of us talk about reparations,

01:19:48.940 --> 01:19:50.970
but we don't even have all,

01:19:50.970 --> 01:19:53.160
every Black person in this country

01:19:53.160 --> 01:19:55.310
feel like who should get reparation?

01:19:55.310 --> 01:19:56.367
What does reparation look like?

01:19:56.367 --> 01:19:58.390
You know, if you from Jamaica,

01:19:58.390 --> 01:20:00.060
some folk don't think you
should get reparation.

01:20:00.060 --> 01:20:02.930
I'm like, "My God, when did
that start happening?" Right.

01:20:02.930 --> 01:20:06.340
So we got a lot of work to
do to just bring us together.

01:20:06.340 --> 01:20:08.050
But I do find this to be a both end,

01:20:08.050 --> 01:20:10.100
at least in how I'm hearing the question.

01:20:11.640 --> 01:20:13.720
- Yeah, I think that's true
of this conversation too.

01:20:13.720 --> 01:20:15.450
Black folks don't all agree, right.

01:20:15.450 --> 01:20:18.040
If people don't all agree on
the starting point, right.

01:20:18.040 --> 01:20:20.420
And so what I see that
question as a starting point

01:20:20.420 --> 01:20:22.420
in the same way that perhaps
talking about the body

01:20:22.420 --> 01:20:23.253
of research,

01:20:23.253 --> 01:20:25.120
I think the body of
research on reparations

01:20:25.120 --> 01:20:26.210
is building up as well.

01:20:26.210 --> 01:20:31.210
I think Sandy William
Darity work on reparations

01:20:31.437 --> 01:20:34.630
and the distinct kind of
the justifications for it

01:20:34.630 --> 01:20:37.430
and how it might play out
is one example of that.

01:20:37.430 --> 01:20:39.970
I just wanna go back to one point

01:20:39.970 --> 01:20:43.620
and Brandon, you might, you
know, because of your expertise,

01:20:43.620 --> 01:20:46.860
you can help me think
about this, but the idea,

01:20:46.860 --> 01:20:49.140
I don't think that you're
saying that Black people

01:20:49.140 --> 01:20:52.490
haven't been engaged with
the making of the state,

01:20:52.490 --> 01:20:54.280
or maybe distinguishing
between Black people

01:20:54.280 --> 01:20:55.220
and Blackness, right.

01:20:55.220 --> 01:20:56.990
Because we know the state as it exists,

01:20:56.990 --> 01:20:59.510
is in response in part to Blackness

01:20:59.510 --> 01:21:02.070
and the understanding of that, right.

01:21:02.070 --> 01:21:06.430
And so the question for me
is how is it that the demands

01:21:06.430 --> 01:21:08.750
that are being made are incorporated

01:21:08.750 --> 01:21:13.360
into not just a state, but
that a state that is, you know,

01:21:13.360 --> 01:21:14.830
when you think about
abolitionist imagining

01:21:14.830 --> 01:21:18.690
can actually create the
kinds of institutions

01:21:18.690 --> 01:21:22.030
that are necessary for
the population of people

01:21:22.030 --> 01:21:24.010
we have within these borders now,

01:21:24.010 --> 01:21:25.740
while also accounting, right

01:21:25.740 --> 01:21:29.340
for the sovereign territory
and the unseated territory

01:21:29.340 --> 01:21:30.173
and all that.

01:21:30.173 --> 01:21:33.790
So that's a big question,
but thinking, you know,

01:21:33.790 --> 01:21:36.570
acknowledging the fact that
even in this conversation,

01:21:36.570 --> 01:21:39.300
there is a possibility
to remake the state,

01:21:39.300 --> 01:21:41.600
but it can't be on the
conditions of the state.

01:21:41.600 --> 01:21:44.060
And that's why I keep coming
back to this point that,

01:21:44.060 --> 01:21:48.350
that we have to confront directly
the violence of the state.

01:21:48.350 --> 01:21:51.160
You can't kind of, you
know, move around it.

01:21:51.160 --> 01:21:53.460
And what does that mean
in a very material sense

01:21:53.460 --> 01:21:55.590
to take Pastor Mike's point?

01:21:55.590 --> 01:21:58.620
That means that when a young
man tells me that he grew up

01:21:58.620 --> 01:22:01.730
with the hands of police
officers down his pants,

01:22:01.730 --> 01:22:04.540
as a consequence of this war on drugs,

01:22:04.540 --> 01:22:06.580
that was a war on young people,

01:22:06.580 --> 01:22:09.987
my politics, my practice has
to get in the way of that.

01:22:09.987 --> 01:22:12.240
Has to interrupt that, has to disrupt that

01:22:12.240 --> 01:22:15.030
because whatever you
think about crime rates

01:22:15.030 --> 01:22:17.810
and bringing them down
that cannot be a way

01:22:17.810 --> 01:22:19.820
in which we do it, right.

01:22:19.820 --> 01:22:22.078
And so, and we know like just the history

01:22:22.078 --> 01:22:26.410
of Black feminist thought
shows us the many, many ways

01:22:26.410 --> 01:22:29.130
that Black women have
cared for one another,

01:22:29.130 --> 01:22:31.580
cared for the community, right.

01:22:31.580 --> 01:22:33.868
And so that we know those
very material practices

01:22:33.868 --> 01:22:36.600
in a tradition of that exists.

01:22:36.600 --> 01:22:39.980
And so, you know, I think
it is this both end,

01:22:39.980 --> 01:22:43.920
but we cannot get out
of this where we are,

01:22:43.920 --> 01:22:46.500
we cannot do this
remaking, this reimagining,

01:22:46.500 --> 01:22:50.790
this transformation without
a direct confrontation.

01:22:50.790 --> 01:22:55.090
Confrontation which will feel
antagonistic to some folks

01:22:55.090 --> 01:22:56.423
with state violence.

01:22:58.260 --> 01:23:00.040
- Yeah, I don't disagree with that.

01:23:00.040 --> 01:23:04.749
I mean, I should be more
precise where I think,

01:23:04.749 --> 01:23:07.130
you know, look, we have an
enormously rich tradition.

01:23:07.130 --> 01:23:10.520
I've devoted my life to studying
Black political thought.

01:23:10.520 --> 01:23:13.810
I think like any genre
of political thought,

01:23:13.810 --> 01:23:17.060
there are things that traditions fixate on

01:23:17.060 --> 01:23:18.223
because they have to.

01:23:19.680 --> 01:23:22.140
So, you see in other traditions,

01:23:22.140 --> 01:23:25.530
like if you take kinda
19th and 20th century

01:23:25.530 --> 01:23:27.290
German political thought,

01:23:27.290 --> 01:23:31.313
you just see a lot of focus on
how to develop a bureaucracy,

01:23:32.170 --> 01:23:34.823
'cause they're engaged in
the process of doing that.

01:23:35.670 --> 01:23:39.290
Not a lot of our thinkers
were allowed to participate

01:23:39.290 --> 01:23:42.500
in the act of construction
of the bureaucracies

01:23:42.500 --> 01:23:44.480
that we live under,

01:23:44.480 --> 01:23:46.590
we have a much richer tradition

01:23:46.590 --> 01:23:47.987
of how to think about
the ethics of protests

01:23:47.987 --> 01:23:49.790
and the ethics of resistance.

01:23:49.790 --> 01:23:51.260
So that's all I was just trying to say

01:23:51.260 --> 01:23:55.470
is that for our generation of thinkers,

01:23:55.470 --> 01:23:59.180
one of the difficult moves
is going to be, you know,

01:23:59.180 --> 01:24:04.180
coming down on this question
of do we need to develop

01:24:05.823 --> 01:24:08.240
more more sustained forms of state craft

01:24:08.240 --> 01:24:10.010
and how can we rethink the state?

01:24:10.010 --> 01:24:11.853
What is our conception of the state?

01:24:14.240 --> 01:24:16.150
And what might state
institutions look like?

01:24:16.150 --> 01:24:18.520
I was using the progressive
era as an analogy

01:24:18.520 --> 01:24:21.250
because it's one of the
moments in American history

01:24:21.250 --> 01:24:24.460
where left liberals spend
a lot of time thinking

01:24:24.460 --> 01:24:27.320
about how the state might
operate very differently

01:24:27.320 --> 01:24:29.110
than it did prior, right.

01:24:29.110 --> 01:24:31.650
Getting rid of patronage appointments,

01:24:31.650 --> 01:24:34.770
trying to think about the
spoil system, things like that.

01:24:34.770 --> 01:24:39.770
So just one quick example, you know,

01:24:40.130 --> 01:24:42.363
do we have a unitary
conception of the state

01:24:42.363 --> 01:24:44.410
that we're operating with, right?

01:24:44.410 --> 01:24:47.490
Or is the state a jumble
of competing interest

01:24:47.490 --> 01:24:49.380
in the institutions,

01:24:49.380 --> 01:24:51.820
which we might see some element

01:24:51.820 --> 01:24:55.723
of and use them as a counter
power to parts of the state

01:24:55.723 --> 01:24:58.090
that we're antagonistic toward.

01:24:58.090 --> 01:25:00.960
That's just an open
political theory question

01:25:00.960 --> 01:25:03.930
that I don't think we have a
lot of debate about right now

01:25:03.930 --> 01:25:06.623
in the space of public safety.

01:25:08.280 --> 01:25:09.460
So I wanted to say that.

01:25:09.460 --> 01:25:10.580
And then the second thing,

01:25:10.580 --> 01:25:13.590
just more directly to Pastor Mike's point,

01:25:13.590 --> 01:25:16.060
which I think is really important.

01:25:16.060 --> 01:25:18.290
So how do we convince people?

01:25:18.290 --> 01:25:20.080
What are the breadcrumbs, you know,

01:25:20.080 --> 01:25:22.023
use the analogy to breadcrumbs.

01:25:23.010 --> 01:25:27.480
It's like a lot of my work
right now is about narratives,

01:25:27.480 --> 01:25:28.890
narratives of history.

01:25:28.890 --> 01:25:33.620
And one of my deep
concerns is the popularity

01:25:33.620 --> 01:25:35.290
of what I call ironic narratives,

01:25:35.290 --> 01:25:37.930
but other people might
call pessimist narratives

01:25:37.930 --> 01:25:39.330
or Afro-pessimist narratives

01:25:40.170 --> 01:25:45.170
that really diminish the
significance of partial victories

01:25:46.130 --> 01:25:50.723
and treat them as elusory,
meaningless, non-events,

01:25:53.770 --> 01:25:56.030
I think that's really demoralizing

01:25:56.030 --> 01:26:00.220
to a social movement struggle
to the kind of practice

01:26:00.220 --> 01:26:03.040
that Du Bois called the "long siege,"

01:26:03.040 --> 01:26:04.870
which I think is would any struggle

01:26:04.870 --> 01:26:06.953
against the history of White
supremacy in this country,

01:26:06.953 --> 01:26:09.990
and the current practice of
White supremacy in this country

01:26:09.990 --> 01:26:11.410
is gonna look like.

01:26:11.410 --> 01:26:13.650
And I think we have to be really careful

01:26:13.650 --> 01:26:16.230
that although such narratives
can feel cathartic,

01:26:16.230 --> 01:26:18.620
'cause they allow us to
denounce all existence

01:26:18.620 --> 01:26:21.810
in one sweep that they
can be really demoralizing

01:26:21.810 --> 01:26:23.280
on the ground.

01:26:23.280 --> 01:26:25.550
So that's one thing I
think we have to avoid

01:26:25.550 --> 01:26:27.940
or narratives that
diminish partial victories.

01:26:27.940 --> 01:26:30.810
We need to find ways of
lifting up partial victories

01:26:30.810 --> 01:26:33.623
while still acknowledging
how partial they are.

01:26:34.690 --> 01:26:36.370
And then the other thing I was gonna say

01:26:36.370 --> 01:26:38.990
is that from African-American history,

01:26:38.990 --> 01:26:42.020
I always I'm enamored with, you know,

01:26:42.020 --> 01:26:44.330
I'm teaching class on Malcolm X now,

01:26:44.330 --> 01:26:46.610
I always teach classes
on a Black Power movement

01:26:46.610 --> 01:26:49.150
and think a lot about the Panthers.

01:26:49.150 --> 01:26:50.940
And I think for everyone

01:26:53.420 --> 01:26:56.020
who struggles with the
history of Black radicalism,

01:26:56.020 --> 01:26:57.540
one of the things that's really appealing

01:26:57.540 --> 01:26:58.840
about those two movements,

01:26:58.840 --> 01:27:00.130
the movement around Malcolm X

01:27:00.130 --> 01:27:01.720
and the movement around the Panthers

01:27:01.720 --> 01:27:03.860
is that they were able to get people

01:27:03.860 --> 01:27:06.930
who are involved in the perpetration

01:27:06.930 --> 01:27:10.890
of public space violence to participate

01:27:12.000 --> 01:27:13.693
actively in political struggle.

01:27:14.850 --> 01:27:17.930
And to me that just is
still the holy grail.

01:27:17.930 --> 01:27:22.930
That's still proof of concept
that can you show people

01:27:23.000 --> 01:27:24.090
in Black communities,

01:27:24.090 --> 01:27:27.760
poor of Black communities
that you can reach folks

01:27:27.760 --> 01:27:30.880
who everyone else thinks nobody can reach?

01:27:30.880 --> 01:27:32.290
And that need to be thrown away,

01:27:32.290 --> 01:27:34.940
need to be locked away incapacitated.

01:27:34.940 --> 01:27:36.680
When you can reach those people

01:27:36.680 --> 01:27:40.010
and show their political moral agency,

01:27:40.010 --> 01:27:43.240
their genius, their creativity,

01:27:43.240 --> 01:27:45.970
I think there's just
something still spectacular

01:27:45.970 --> 01:27:48.760
and compelling and persuasive
about that project,

01:27:48.760 --> 01:27:50.653
but it is extraordinarily difficult.

01:27:53.110 --> 01:27:55.660
- I really I'm, at this point,

01:27:55.660 --> 01:27:58.400
I went from frustrated to being angry

01:27:58.400 --> 01:28:01.430
that this conversation can't go longer.

01:28:01.430 --> 01:28:02.400
I wanna respect folks time.

01:28:02.400 --> 01:28:04.350
We're coming up on about 14 minutes left

01:28:04.350 --> 01:28:06.030
and I swore to all the panelists,

01:28:06.030 --> 01:28:08.460
they were having a chance
to do closing remarks.

01:28:08.460 --> 01:28:10.902
So we're gonna go through
those closing remarks.

01:28:10.902 --> 01:28:13.590
Part of me wants to know about what is,

01:28:13.590 --> 01:28:14.740
and isn't new?

01:28:14.740 --> 01:28:16.870
Brandon you talked about partial victories

01:28:16.870 --> 01:28:19.080
and I thought I was in
Pastor Mike's church

01:28:19.080 --> 01:28:21.190
when I was saying, amen to it,

01:28:21.190 --> 01:28:23.780
the tensions between what we can get today

01:28:23.780 --> 01:28:25.600
versus what we must demand for tomorrow,

01:28:25.600 --> 01:28:27.150
which are clearly be part
of this long struggle,

01:28:27.150 --> 01:28:29.020
but feel like they're having
a different conversation

01:28:29.020 --> 01:28:29.853
right now.

01:28:29.853 --> 01:28:32.420
All of that, but I'll
leave with this prompt

01:28:32.420 --> 01:28:35.170
and then ask folks to take
just two to three minutes

01:28:35.170 --> 01:28:37.330
to wrap up, respond to
the prompt or don't.

01:28:37.330 --> 01:28:38.990
But the prompt coming in, again,

01:28:38.990 --> 01:28:42.260
as a synthesis from a couple
of the direct messages,

01:28:42.260 --> 01:28:44.830
we've talked, this is a
conversation framed around

01:28:44.830 --> 01:28:48.523
what organizers and activists
can do in this moment.

01:28:49.982 --> 01:28:54.430
And if you could name the
one thing, the one thing,

01:28:54.430 --> 01:28:58.860
the one constraint on activism right now,

01:28:58.860 --> 01:29:01.200
the one constraint that if we removed it

01:29:02.060 --> 01:29:04.380
would be the biggest lever
on change that we could do.

01:29:04.380 --> 01:29:06.240
The one constraint that could be removed.

01:29:06.240 --> 01:29:07.240
Go ahead and name that,

01:29:07.240 --> 01:29:08.360
and then go ahead and wrap up.

01:29:08.360 --> 01:29:10.995
We're gonna go in the same
order, Dr. Nikki Jones,

01:29:10.995 --> 01:29:14.899
if you wanna go ahead and start us off.

01:29:14.899 --> 01:29:19.899
- Oh, (Nikki chuckles) there
is no one thing, right?

01:29:20.830 --> 01:29:24.930
We are operating in a structural context

01:29:24.930 --> 01:29:29.930
that positions activists and
organizers and there's overlap

01:29:30.350 --> 01:29:33.800
and there's distinctions in certain ways,

01:29:33.800 --> 01:29:36.250
has them in a place.

01:29:36.250 --> 01:29:41.190
And that that is built
in to the work, right?

01:29:41.190 --> 01:29:44.350
That has been the long struggle.

01:29:44.350 --> 01:29:46.110
I'm not a pessimist

01:29:46.110 --> 01:29:51.110
and yet I don't see that
changing in the near future.

01:29:51.840 --> 01:29:55.870
What I know is happening
is that the people

01:29:55.870 --> 01:29:58.870
who were instigating
and motivating the work

01:29:58.870 --> 01:30:02.040
that led to the uprisings of summer 2020

01:30:02.040 --> 01:30:06.030
have not led up and they're
continuing to do that work.

01:30:06.030 --> 01:30:09.850
And what becomes transformative
in any moment of rupture

01:30:09.850 --> 01:30:14.720
is the set of ideas that
percolate to the surface.

01:30:14.720 --> 01:30:17.920
And that's why this
rupture in summer 2020,

01:30:17.920 --> 01:30:22.080
between reform and
abolition was so significant

01:30:22.080 --> 01:30:23.920
and remains so consequential.

01:30:23.920 --> 01:30:27.230
So even defund as a slogan,
didn't emerge in that moment,

01:30:27.230 --> 01:30:29.070
as sure as folks here appreciate,

01:30:29.070 --> 01:30:33.435
it emerged in Oakland years before, right?

01:30:33.435 --> 01:30:37.140
In response to a very material problem.

01:30:37.140 --> 01:30:39.360
And then that leads to the cert...

01:30:39.360 --> 01:30:41.890
And the reaction to that
is what leads to change.

01:30:41.890 --> 01:30:43.100
We don't wanna defund the police,

01:30:43.100 --> 01:30:48.020
but maybe we'll think
about relocating resources.

01:30:48.020 --> 01:30:50.520
Maybe we'll think about a
department of public safety

01:30:50.520 --> 01:30:51.353
in Minneapolis,

01:30:51.353 --> 01:30:53.440
which got 43% of the
vote which is not nothing

01:30:53.440 --> 01:30:54.520
and probably before this moment,

01:30:54.520 --> 01:30:57.380
wouldn't have gotten
12% of the vote, right?

01:30:57.380 --> 01:30:59.630
And so I just wanna bring our attention

01:30:59.630 --> 01:31:03.460
to the fact that this work is happening.

01:31:03.460 --> 01:31:05.860
This change is happening,

01:31:05.860 --> 01:31:08.470
and the people who are
closest to the problem

01:31:08.470 --> 01:31:10.040
are the ones who are figuring it out.

01:31:10.040 --> 01:31:13.900
The question for the we that
exists here in some respect.

01:31:13.900 --> 01:31:16.870
And I'll think I'll say
something specifically

01:31:16.870 --> 01:31:21.870
about the we in the academy
is to acknowledge our role

01:31:21.930 --> 01:31:24.080
in producing the conditions

01:31:24.080 --> 01:31:25.440
that we have talked about

01:31:25.440 --> 01:31:28.210
over the course of our
time together today,

01:31:28.210 --> 01:31:32.270
and to rethink how we
reallocate our resources, right?

01:31:32.270 --> 01:31:36.740
So that we are funneling
the power of the university

01:31:36.740 --> 01:31:37.970
to those who need it most.

01:31:37.970 --> 01:31:42.970
So to me, I see that as the
kind of, you know, the practice,

01:31:43.320 --> 01:31:44.500
the liberatory practice,

01:31:44.500 --> 01:31:47.090
the fusion practice that
we need to be invested

01:31:47.090 --> 01:31:52.090
in this moment and that is
actually most sensitive, right.

01:31:52.450 --> 01:31:54.800
And has the kind of most accurate
reading of the conditions

01:31:54.800 --> 01:31:56.773
in which we find ourselves.

01:31:57.840 --> 01:31:58.673
I'll stop there.

01:32:02.510 --> 01:32:03.860
- Thanks, thank you.

01:32:03.860 --> 01:32:05.373
Yeah, Dr. Pat Sharkey.

01:32:07.990 --> 01:32:11.640
- So I've learned when I
should sit out a question

01:32:11.640 --> 01:32:16.410
and listen and I think this is a question

01:32:16.410 --> 01:32:19.500
that where it's better for me to listen

01:32:19.500 --> 01:32:20.720
than anything else,

01:32:20.720 --> 01:32:24.980
but what I will say is
that as following up

01:32:24.980 --> 01:32:29.593
on Nikki's powerful point
about what role academics play.

01:32:30.520 --> 01:32:33.240
One of the things that I've learned

01:32:33.240 --> 01:32:35.980
about particularly my role,

01:32:35.980 --> 01:32:37.920
what a White academic can play,

01:32:37.920 --> 01:32:40.520
who doesn't do the work on the ground,

01:32:40.520 --> 01:32:43.860
who doesn't do the
ethnography that Nikki does,

01:32:43.860 --> 01:32:47.460
who does quantitative
work to try to understand

01:32:48.523 --> 01:32:51.243
these challenges we're talking about?

01:32:53.080 --> 01:32:57.900
I've found that my job
is to relentlessly focus

01:32:57.900 --> 01:33:02.900
on explanation, relentlessly
move past the observation

01:33:04.230 --> 01:33:07.810
of inequality, the
description of inequality

01:33:07.810 --> 01:33:10.280
and toward explanation.

01:33:10.280 --> 01:33:15.280
And that's why I go to
Elizabeth Hinton's work

01:33:15.670 --> 01:33:19.810
and why it's so powerful
because it gives academics

01:33:19.810 --> 01:33:22.750
the tools to say, okay,
here's a challenge.

01:33:22.750 --> 01:33:26.230
Now let's understand where this arose

01:33:26.230 --> 01:33:28.500
and what were the intentional policies

01:33:28.500 --> 01:33:31.070
and what were the
impacts of those policies

01:33:31.070 --> 01:33:32.270
and those social movements

01:33:32.270 --> 01:33:36.803
that led to this form of
inequality that we're now seeing?

01:33:37.770 --> 01:33:41.360
And so I'll stop there and just say,

01:33:41.360 --> 01:33:45.060
I see my job as an academic
to just relentlessly focus

01:33:45.060 --> 01:33:48.080
on explanation and never stop

01:33:48.080 --> 01:33:50.200
until I have made the empirical point

01:33:50.200 --> 01:33:51.943
about what led to this point.

01:33:54.150 --> 01:33:55.140
- Thank you, Pat.

01:33:55.140 --> 01:33:57.630
Dr. Brandon Terry final thoughts.

01:33:57.630 --> 01:34:00.323
- Yeah, well, this is
a really hard question.

01:34:02.720 --> 01:34:07.720
And I think from me the biggest obstacle,

01:34:08.310 --> 01:34:11.030
and I think the biggest
thing that's changed

01:34:11.030 --> 01:34:15.760
since the last major
wave of Black radicalism

01:34:15.760 --> 01:34:18.830
in the late 1960s and early 1970s

01:34:18.830 --> 01:34:20.770
is the carceral state itself.

01:34:20.770 --> 01:34:25.770
I mean, we've just taken a lot
of brilliant, hard working,

01:34:29.540 --> 01:34:34.540
interesting people out of
circulation and public space.

01:34:35.770 --> 01:34:40.200
And unlike the era of the
great prison movements

01:34:40.200 --> 01:34:43.360
of the late 1960s and early 1970s

01:34:43.360 --> 01:34:48.080
is also really, really
difficult to organize in prisons

01:34:49.179 --> 01:34:52.040
in ways, I mean, it was
always difficult to organize

01:34:52.040 --> 01:34:54.420
in prisons, but it's
become in some respects,

01:34:54.420 --> 01:34:55.390
even more difficult.

01:34:55.390 --> 01:34:57.890
People can't even get their
hands on books to read

01:34:59.610 --> 01:35:04.190
without severe censorship and
surveillance and repression.

01:35:04.190 --> 01:35:06.660
And these things have
only gotten more intense

01:35:06.660 --> 01:35:08.030
in the wake of the pandemic

01:35:08.030 --> 01:35:10.950
where there's been really harsh lockdowns

01:35:10.950 --> 01:35:12.433
and lots of facilities.

01:35:13.420 --> 01:35:15.870
And so one of the problems that creates

01:35:15.870 --> 01:35:20.660
is that it really
intensifies the disconnect

01:35:20.660 --> 01:35:23.410
that Pastor Mike was speaking about,

01:35:23.410 --> 01:35:27.230
of people trying to connect
the lived experience

01:35:27.230 --> 01:35:30.177
of people most susceptible to,

01:35:30.177 --> 01:35:32.400
and most likely to perpetrate

01:35:32.400 --> 01:35:34.960
what we're calling community violence

01:35:34.960 --> 01:35:37.460
and the politics that emerge from it.

01:35:37.460 --> 01:35:39.680
And we haven't really
been able to develop,

01:35:39.680 --> 01:35:41.260
there's some great experiments in this,

01:35:41.260 --> 01:35:44.510
but there hasn't been a strategy

01:35:44.510 --> 01:35:47.540
that quite seems scalable as of yet

01:35:47.540 --> 01:35:52.360
about how to overcome that
the repressive function

01:35:52.360 --> 01:35:57.360
of the carceral state on
radical politics in this moment.

01:35:57.370 --> 01:35:59.130
So that would be the biggest thing.

01:35:59.130 --> 01:36:02.430
And I think as academics, you know,

01:36:02.430 --> 01:36:07.070
one of the things we can
contribute to that project

01:36:07.070 --> 01:36:11.250
is really doing the hard
work of normative analysis

01:36:11.250 --> 01:36:13.770
to try to, you know, the
old work of critical theory,

01:36:13.770 --> 01:36:17.460
clarifying the struggles of the age.

01:36:17.460 --> 01:36:20.420
Like let's really clarify
what people are arguing,

01:36:20.420 --> 01:36:22.550
why they're saying things are unjust?

01:36:22.550 --> 01:36:24.510
What kinds of norms they're appealing to?

01:36:24.510 --> 01:36:27.980
What are the differences
between duties, goods,

01:36:27.980 --> 01:36:31.020
and just prudential considerations?

01:36:31.020 --> 01:36:31.990
Why those might matter?

01:36:31.990 --> 01:36:35.740
Because they help open up
the space of persuasion

01:36:35.740 --> 01:36:40.353
for allies we're asking to risk a lot

01:36:40.353 --> 01:36:45.230
in the struggles against authoritarianism,

01:36:45.230 --> 01:36:46.700
whether it takes the form of the prison

01:36:46.700 --> 01:36:48.240
or whether it takes the form

01:36:48.240 --> 01:36:49.860
of the anti-democratic movements

01:36:49.860 --> 01:36:51.333
we're seeing insurgent today.

01:36:54.670 --> 01:36:55.684
- Thank you, Brandon.

01:36:55.684 --> 01:36:57.943
And last, but certainly
not least Pastor Mike.

01:37:00.390 --> 01:37:02.730
- You know, this question
about what is one thing

01:37:02.730 --> 01:37:04.473
I'm like Dr. Jones,

01:37:05.460 --> 01:37:09.880
you know, when I say God, I
mean, Father, Son, Holy Spirit,

01:37:09.880 --> 01:37:12.190
we gotta start at least with
three things a good preacher,

01:37:12.190 --> 01:37:16.860
you know, in me, but I do
worry about isolationism,

01:37:16.860 --> 01:37:21.860
you know, like that we can become isolated

01:37:22.920 --> 01:37:27.520
in our silos by just the rigor of the work

01:37:27.520 --> 01:37:29.630
that we are all called to do uniquely.

01:37:29.630 --> 01:37:34.340
And that isolationism creates
a level of incompatibility

01:37:37.440 --> 01:37:39.820
over time where we just can't even,

01:37:39.820 --> 01:37:42.060
we can say the same word,
but not mean the same thing.

01:37:42.060 --> 01:37:47.010
And so, you know, I wonder
like, can we walk together?

01:37:47.010 --> 01:37:48.640
How can we walk together?

01:37:48.640 --> 01:37:51.100
You know, I'm finding this
to be the biggest challenge

01:37:51.100 --> 01:37:52.600
in our movement space,

01:37:52.600 --> 01:37:54.627
post the Ferguson uprising, et cetera,

01:37:54.627 --> 01:37:55.460
"Black Lives Matter,"

01:37:55.460 --> 01:37:56.870
however you wanna describe it.

01:37:59.000 --> 01:38:00.890
A lot of us aren't
walking together anymore

01:38:00.890 --> 01:38:03.180
and that's just within
one part of the movement.

01:38:03.180 --> 01:38:06.300
Like how can we walk together as you know,

01:38:06.300 --> 01:38:09.750
across disciplines, across
the academy, across religious,

01:38:09.750 --> 01:38:13.240
you know, movements,
across elected officials,

01:38:13.240 --> 01:38:16.570
it's like how can we walk together?

01:38:16.570 --> 01:38:19.410
And it is frightening for me

01:38:19.410 --> 01:38:22.650
to think about these
authoritarian impulses

01:38:22.650 --> 01:38:26.980
sweeping the country, and we
who are in its cross hairs,

01:38:26.980 --> 01:38:29.600
can't figure out how to walk together,

01:38:29.600 --> 01:38:31.150
not in some kumbaya our way,

01:38:31.150 --> 01:38:33.870
but just in a relational manner

01:38:33.870 --> 01:38:37.340
where we can have a conversation over time

01:38:37.340 --> 01:38:41.410
and do the dreaming that we
all say we want to do, right.

01:38:41.410 --> 01:38:43.370
It's not to me about intentions,

01:38:43.370 --> 01:38:46.820
it's just about how can we
just functionally walk together

01:38:46.820 --> 01:38:49.267
this capitalist economy that makes us,

01:38:49.267 --> 01:38:51.070
I have to write papers, preach message,

01:38:51.070 --> 01:38:53.280
like, you know, I gotta get a new thing

01:38:53.280 --> 01:38:54.870
to say every week, right?

01:38:54.870 --> 01:38:57.060
The same message every
week differently, right?

01:38:57.060 --> 01:38:59.150
A capitalistic endeavor
somebody's doing there, right.

01:38:59.150 --> 01:39:01.380
Or I figure out a new organizing plan

01:39:01.380 --> 01:39:03.420
every week, every month.

01:39:03.420 --> 01:39:05.310
And that work in and of itself,

01:39:05.310 --> 01:39:07.350
it I think often keeps our relationships

01:39:07.350 --> 01:39:12.350
from being little deep and life giving.

01:39:13.370 --> 01:39:14.670
So that's what I would say.

01:39:14.670 --> 01:39:18.400
And I do believe we have
massive opportunities,

01:39:18.400 --> 01:39:20.370
but it is still early.

01:39:20.370 --> 01:39:23.620
These same opportunities
could become Trojan horses

01:39:23.620 --> 01:39:28.030
to continue to fund the
state, the incarcerated state,

01:39:28.030 --> 01:39:29.470
the police state,

01:39:29.470 --> 01:39:31.520
all these things that
we know are problematic.

01:39:31.520 --> 01:39:35.230
And so I do hope that as we continue

01:39:35.230 --> 01:39:37.113
to lean into this moment,

01:39:37.980 --> 01:39:40.433
we can maximize these opportunities

01:39:40.433 --> 01:39:45.433
to both recreate, but also
dismantle those things

01:39:45.740 --> 01:39:47.550
that cause us harm.

01:39:47.550 --> 01:39:50.647
Thanks for having me though,
Dr. Phil and everybody.

01:39:51.520 --> 01:39:53.350
- See, I thought we were so close.

01:39:53.350 --> 01:39:55.240
We were so close to getting
outta one session together

01:39:55.240 --> 01:39:58.517
with no Dr. Phil references
and there at the very end,

01:39:58.517 --> 01:40:00.543
(Philip laughs) my brother,

01:40:03.340 --> 01:40:07.010
this has been such a
nourishing conversation.

01:40:07.010 --> 01:40:08.400
Thanks the four of y'all.

01:40:08.400 --> 01:40:09.480
Before I let folks go,

01:40:09.480 --> 01:40:11.070
I wanna make sure that we
get a special shout out

01:40:11.070 --> 01:40:13.830
to Vanessa Epps who helped
get all the logistics

01:40:13.830 --> 01:40:14.860
of this together.

01:40:14.860 --> 01:40:17.540
Make sure that our panelists were here.

01:40:17.540 --> 01:40:20.150
You know she made all of us
look significantly better

01:40:20.150 --> 01:40:21.290
than we would have otherwise.

01:40:21.290 --> 01:40:23.030
So thank you so much, Vanessa.

01:40:23.030 --> 01:40:25.200
Thank you all for coming.

01:40:25.200 --> 01:40:28.650
I cannot wait for the next and
the next of these endeavors,

01:40:28.650 --> 01:40:29.700
but please join me.

01:40:29.700 --> 01:40:30.533
Raise your hands,

01:40:30.533 --> 01:40:33.670
take yourselves off mute
and thanking our panelists

01:40:33.670 --> 01:40:35.850
for sharing their wisdom and
their brilliance with us today.

01:40:35.850 --> 01:40:38.970
Thank y'all so, so, so much.

01:40:38.970 --> 01:40:40.440
- Thank you.

01:40:40.440 --> 01:40:41.640
- Thank you so much.

01:40:41.640 --> 01:40:42.751
- Thank you.

01:40:42.751 --> 01:40:43.751
- Thank you.

